(globalThis.Cuecode ??= {}).data = {"toolchains":{"python":"Python 3.14.2","swift":"Swift 6.4","java":"Java 21.0.7","cobol":"GnuCOBOL 3.2.0","js":"Node 23.5.0","ts":"TypeScript 5.9.3"},"checkedOn":"2026-11-05","concepts":[{"id":"hello","docs":["compiler","interpreter"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"One line. Nothing else needed.","code":"print(\"Hello, world!\")","output":"Hello, world!","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"One line too, when it's a script.","code":"print(\"Hello, world!\")","output":"Hello, world!","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"Everything lives in a class, and the program starts at `main`.","code":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(\"Hello, world!\");\n    }\n}","output":"Hello, world!","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","note":"Divisions come first. The dots end sentences, and they matter.","code":"       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.\n       PROGRAM-ID. HELLO.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           DISPLAY \"Hello, world!\".\n           STOP RUN.","output":"Hello, world!","checked":"ran"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"In a browser, this shows up in the developer console, not on the page.","code":"console.log(\"Hello, world!\");","output":"Hello, world!","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"full","note":"JavaScript plus labels that say what kind of thing each value is.","code":"const message: string = \"Hello, world!\";\nconsole.log(message);","output":"Hello, world!","checked":"ran"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"live","note":"HTML doesn't run. It describes what's on the page.","code":"<!doctype html>\n<title>Hello</title>\n<h1>Hello, world!</h1>","checked":"live"},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"live","note":"CSS can't say anything itself. It styles what HTML already put there.","demo":"<h1>Hello, world!</h1>","code":"h1 {\n  color: tomato;\n  font-family: Georgia, serif;\n}","checked":"live"}},"title":"Say hello","group":"Basics","blurb":"Make the computer show a line of text.","explain":"Every language has a way to put text in front of you. It's the first thing to learn because it's how you see what your program is doing.\n\nLook at how much each language asks for before it will say one word. Python and JavaScript need one line. Java wants a class and a `main` method. COBOL wants a whole structure.","mainframe":"`DISPLAY` doesn't go to a screen. A batch job has no screen. The text lands in the job's output (SYSOUT), and you read it later in SDSF. COBOL's layout also comes from punched cards: code starts at column 8, and nothing past column 72 counts."},{"id":"variables","docs":["variable","type","constant"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"No types written down. The value decides.","code":"name = \"Asha\"\nage = 34\nprice = 19.99\nactive = True\n\nage = age + 1\nprint(name, age, price, active)","output":"Asha 35 19.99 True","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"`let` can't change, `var` can. Swift works out the type for you.","code":"let name = \"Asha\"\nvar age = 34\nlet price = 19.99\nlet active = true\n\nage = age + 1\nprint(name, age, price, active)","output":"Asha 35 19.99 true","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"The type comes first, every time.","code":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String name = \"Asha\";\n        int age = 34;\n        double price = 19.99;\n        boolean active = true;\n\n        age = age + 1;\n        System.out.println(name + \" \" + age + \" \" + price + \" \" + active);\n    }\n}","output":"Asha 35 19.99 true","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","note":"The output shows the real size of each box: padded name, 035 with its zero.","code":"       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.\n       PROGRAM-ID. VARS.\n       DATA DIVISION.\n       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.\n       01  WS-NAME      PIC X(10)    VALUE \"Asha\".\n       01  WS-AGE       PIC 9(3)     VALUE 34.\n       01  WS-PRICE     PIC 9(3)V99  VALUE 19.99.\n       01  WS-ACTIVE    PIC X        VALUE \"Y\".\n           88  IS-ACTIVE             VALUE \"Y\".\n       01  WS-PRICE-OUT PIC ZZ9.99.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           ADD 1 TO WS-AGE.\n           MOVE WS-PRICE TO WS-PRICE-OUT.\n           DISPLAY WS-NAME \" \" WS-AGE \" \" WS-PRICE-OUT \" \" WS-ACTIVE.\n           STOP RUN.","output":"Asha       035  19.99 Y","checked":"ran"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"`const` can't be reassigned, `let` can. Avoid the old `var`.","code":"const name = \"Asha\";\nlet age = 34;\nconst price = 19.99;\nconst active = true;\n\nage = age + 1;\nconsole.log(name, age, price, active);","output":"Asha 35 19.99 true","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"full","note":"The same as JavaScript, with types after a colon. They vanish when it runs.","code":"const name: string = \"Asha\";\nlet age: number = 34;\nconst price: number = 19.99;\nconst active: boolean = true;\n\nage = age + 1;\nconsole.log(name, age, price, active);","output":"Asha 35 19.99 true","checked":"ran"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"full","skip":"HTML holds content, not values you can change."},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"live","note":"CSS has variables too. They always start with two dashes.","demo":"<h1>Hello</h1>","code":":root {\n  --brand: tomato;\n}\n\nh1 {\n  color: var(--brand);\n}","checked":"live"}},"title":"Variables and types","group":"Basics","blurb":"Give a value a name so you can use it later.","explain":"A variable is a labelled box. You put a value in, and use the label to get it back.\n\nThe big difference is how strict each language is about what goes in the box. Python and JavaScript let you put anything in. Swift, Java and TypeScript fix the type, so a mistake shows up before the program runs. COBOL goes further: you say exactly how many characters or digits the box holds.","mainframe":"A COBOL variable is a picture of bytes in memory, not an idea of a value. `PIC X(10)` is exactly 10 characters, always, padded with spaces. `PIC 9(3)V99` is 5 digits with an invisible decimal point. Names usually start with `WS-` for Working-Storage. An 88 level gives a yes/no name to a value, so `IF IS-ACTIVE` reads like English."},{"id":"text","docs":["string"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"`f\"...\"` drops values straight into text.","code":"first = \"Grace\"\nlast = \"Hopper\"\nfull = first + \" \" + last\n\nprint(full.upper())\nprint(len(full))\nprint(\"Hop\" in full)\nprint(f\"Hello, {first}!\")","output":"GRACE HOPPER\n12\nTrue\nHello, Grace!","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"`\\(value)` drops values into text. `count` counts real characters, even emoji.","code":"let first = \"Grace\"\nlet last = \"Hopper\"\nlet full = first + \" \" + last\n\nprint(full.uppercased())\nprint(full.count)\nprint(full.contains(\"Hop\"))\nprint(\"Hello, \\(first)!\")","output":"GRACE HOPPER\n12\ntrue\nHello, Grace!","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"Strings never change. `toUpperCase` hands you a new one.","code":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        String first = \"Grace\";\n        String last = \"Hopper\";\n        String full = first + \" \" + last;\n\n        System.out.println(full.toUpperCase());\n        System.out.println(full.length());\n        System.out.println(full.contains(\"Hop\"));\n        System.out.println(\"Hello, %s!\".formatted(first));\n    }\n}","output":"GRACE HOPPER\n12\ntrue\nHello, Grace!","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","note":"Fixed boxes, so \"how long is it?\" needs real work.","code":"       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.\n       PROGRAM-ID. TEXTS.\n       DATA DIVISION.\n       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.\n       01  WS-FIRST    PIC X(10)  VALUE \"Grace\".\n       01  WS-LAST     PIC X(10)  VALUE \"Hopper\".\n       01  WS-FULL     PIC X(21)  VALUE SPACES.\n       01  WS-PAD      PIC 9(2)   VALUE 0.\n       01  WS-LEN      PIC 9(2)   VALUE 0.\n       01  WS-HITS     PIC 9(2)   VALUE 0.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           STRING WS-FIRST DELIMITED BY SPACE\n                  \" \"      DELIMITED BY SIZE\n                  WS-LAST  DELIMITED BY SPACE\n                  INTO WS-FULL\n           END-STRING.\n           DISPLAY FUNCTION UPPER-CASE(WS-FULL).\n           INSPECT FUNCTION REVERSE(WS-FULL)\n               TALLYING WS-PAD FOR LEADING SPACES.\n           COMPUTE WS-LEN = FUNCTION LENGTH(WS-FULL) - WS-PAD.\n           DISPLAY WS-LEN.\n           INSPECT WS-FULL TALLYING WS-HITS FOR ALL \"Hop\".\n           DISPLAY WS-HITS.\n           STOP RUN.","output":"GRACE HOPPER\n12\n01","checked":"ran"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"Backticks make a template string, and `${}` drops values in.","code":"const first = \"Grace\";\nconst last = \"Hopper\";\nconst full = first + \" \" + last;\n\nconsole.log(full.toUpperCase());\nconsole.log(full.length);\nconsole.log(full.includes(\"Hop\"));\nconsole.log(`Hello, ${first}!`);","output":"GRACE HOPPER\n12\ntrue\nHello, Grace!","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"full","note":"Same tools as JavaScript. Here `full.length()` would be caught before running.","code":"const first: string = \"Grace\";\nconst last: string = \"Hopper\";\nconst full: string = first + \" \" + last;\n\nconsole.log(full.toUpperCase());\nconsole.log(full.length);\nconsole.log(full.includes(\"Hop\"));\nconsole.log(`Hello, ${first}!`);","output":"GRACE HOPPER\n12\ntrue\nHello, Grace!","checked":"ran"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"full","skip":"HTML shows text. It can't change it."},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"live","note":"CSS can change how text looks, but the text underneath stays the same.","demo":"<p class=\"name\">Grace Hopper</p>","code":".name {\n  text-transform: uppercase;\n  letter-spacing: 0.08em;\n}","checked":"live"}},"title":"Working with text","group":"Basics","blurb":"Join text, change its case, measure it, search it.","explain":"Text is called a string: a string of characters. Almost every program joins strings, changes them and looks inside them.\n\nMost languages hand you these as one-word tools. Dropping a value into text is called interpolation, and it's the tidy way to build a sentence.","mainframe":"COBOL has no growing strings. Every field has a fixed size and gets padded with spaces, so finding the \"real\" length takes work. `STRING` joins fields and `DELIMITED BY SPACE` stops at the padding. Counting trailing spaces with `FUNCTION REVERSE` is a trick you'll see in real code. Mainframe text is also EBCDIC, not ASCII, so sort order and file transfers can surprise you."},{"id":"numbers","docs":["floating-point","decimal-type","comp-3"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"`/` always gives a decimal, `//` drops the remainder.","code":"from decimal import Decimal\n\nprint(0.1 + 0.2)\nprint(Decimal(\"0.10\") + Decimal(\"0.20\"))\nprint(7 / 2, 7 // 2, 7 % 2)","output":"0.30000000000000004\n0.30\n3.5 3 1","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"Two whole numbers stay whole, so `7 / 2` is 3.","code":"import Foundation\n\nprint(0.1 + 0.2)\nlet a = Decimal(string: \"0.10\")!\nlet b = Decimal(string: \"0.20\")!\nprint(a + b)\nprint(7.0 / 2.0, 7 / 2, 7 % 2)","output":"0.30000000000000004\n0.3\n3.5 3 1","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"Build `BigDecimal` from text. From a double, it's already wrong.","code":"import java.math.BigDecimal;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(0.1 + 0.2);\n        System.out.println(new BigDecimal(\"0.10\").add(new BigDecimal(\"0.20\")));\n        System.out.println(7.0 / 2 + \" \" + 7 / 2 + \" \" + 7 % 2);\n    }\n}","output":"0.30000000000000004\n0.30\n3.5 3 1","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","note":"Exact by default. `COMP-3` is packed decimal, built for money.","code":"       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.\n       PROGRAM-ID. NUMBERS.\n       DATA DIVISION.\n       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.\n       01  WS-A      PIC S9(5)V99 COMP-3 VALUE 0.10.\n       01  WS-B      PIC S9(5)V99 COMP-3 VALUE 0.20.\n       01  WS-SUM    PIC S9(5)V99 COMP-3.\n       01  WS-OUT    PIC ZZ,ZZ9.99.\n       01  WS-Q      PIC 9(3).\n       01  WS-R      PIC 9(3).\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           ADD WS-A TO WS-B GIVING WS-SUM.\n           MOVE WS-SUM TO WS-OUT.\n           DISPLAY WS-OUT.\n           DIVIDE 7 BY 2 GIVING WS-Q REMAINDER WS-R.\n           DISPLAY WS-Q \" \" WS-R.\n           STOP RUN.","output":"     0.30\n003 001","checked":"ran"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"There's only one number type. Keep money in whole cents.","code":"console.log(0.1 + 0.2);\n\nconst cents = 10 + 20;\nconsole.log((cents / 100).toFixed(2));\nconsole.log(7 / 2, Math.floor(7 / 2), 7 % 2);","output":"0.30000000000000004\n0.30\n3.5 3 1","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"full","note":"Types don't change the maths. It's the same as JavaScript.","code":"const cents: number = 10 + 20;\n\nconsole.log(0.1 + 0.2);\nconsole.log((cents / 100).toFixed(2));\nconsole.log(7 / 2, Math.floor(7 / 2), 7 % 2);","output":"0.30000000000000004\n0.30\n3.5 3 1","checked":"ran"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"full","skip":"HTML has no arithmetic."},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"live","note":"`calc()` does sums for sizes, and can mix units.","demo":"<div class=\"bar\">Almost full width</div>","code":".bar {\n  width: calc(100% - 40px);\n  padding: 8px;\n  background: #fde68a;\n}","checked":"live"}},"title":"Numbers and money","group":"Basics","blurb":"Why 0.1 + 0.2 isn't 0.3, and how to count money safely.","explain":"Computers store most decimals in binary, and binary can't hold 0.1 exactly, just as 1/3 can't be written exactly in decimal. So `0.1 + 0.2` gives 0.30000000000000004 in nearly every language.\n\nThat's fine for a game and wrong for a bank. For money you use an exact decimal type, or count in whole cents.\n\nDividing whole numbers also differs: some languages drop the remainder, so `7 / 2` can give 3.","mainframe":"This is why banks still run COBOL. Packed decimal (`COMP-3`) stores money as exact digits, so 0.10 + 0.20 is exactly 0.30, and the hardware does this arithmetic directly. `PIC S9(5)V99` means signed, 5 digits, an implied decimal point, 2 digits. Display fields like `ZZ,ZZ9.99` are only for printing: leading zeros become spaces."},{"id":"if","docs":["condition","boolean"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"Indentation is the structure. `elif` means else-if.","code":"score = 72\n\nif score >= 80:\n    print(\"Great\")\nelif score >= 50:\n    print(\"Pass\")\nelse:\n    print(\"Try again\")","output":"Pass","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"No round brackets needed around the check.","code":"let score = 72\n\nif score >= 80 {\n    print(\"Great\")\n} else if score >= 50 {\n    print(\"Pass\")\n} else {\n    print(\"Try again\")\n}","output":"Pass","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"Round brackets are required.","code":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        int score = 72;\n\n        if (score >= 80) {\n            System.out.println(\"Great\");\n        } else if (score >= 50) {\n            System.out.println(\"Pass\");\n        } else {\n            System.out.println(\"Try again\");\n        }\n    }\n}","output":"Pass","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","note":"`EVALUATE TRUE` reads like a list of cases.","code":"       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.\n       PROGRAM-ID. DECIDE.\n       DATA DIVISION.\n       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.\n       01  WS-SCORE    PIC 9(3)  VALUE 72.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           EVALUATE TRUE\n               WHEN WS-SCORE >= 80\n                   DISPLAY \"Great\"\n               WHEN WS-SCORE >= 50\n                   DISPLAY \"Pass\"\n               WHEN OTHER\n                   DISPLAY \"Try again\"\n           END-EVALUATE.\n           STOP RUN.","output":"Pass","checked":"ran"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"Use `===` to compare. `==` quietly converts types first.","code":"const score = 72;\n\nif (score >= 80) {\n  console.log(\"Great\");\n} else if (score >= 50) {\n  console.log(\"Pass\");\n} else {\n  console.log(\"Try again\");\n}","output":"Pass","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"full","note":"The same as JavaScript, but comparing a number to text is caught early.","code":"const score: number = 72;\n\nif (score >= 80) {\n  console.log(\"Great\");\n} else if (score >= 50) {\n  console.log(\"Pass\");\n} else {\n  console.log(\"Try again\");\n}","output":"Pass","checked":"ran"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"full","skip":"HTML can't choose. It shows what's written."},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"live","note":"The nearest thing CSS has to `if`: apply these rules only on wide screens.","demo":"<p class=\"tip\">Resize the window to change my colour.</p>","code":".tip {\n  color: tomato;\n}\n\n@media (min-width: 600px) {\n  .tip {\n    color: seagreen;\n  }\n}","checked":"live"}},"title":"Making decisions","group":"Basics","blurb":"Do one thing or another, depending on a value.","explain":"A program needs to choose. `if` asks a yes/no question and runs a block when the answer is yes.\n\nChecks run top to bottom and the first match wins. That's why this example asks about 80 before 50.","mainframe":"`EVALUATE TRUE` is COBOL's clean form of if / else if, and you'll see it everywhere. Always close with `END-IF` or `END-EVALUATE`. In old code a single full stop ends every open `IF`, and a stray full stop has caused real production bugs."},{"id":"loops","docs":["loop"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"`range(1, 4)` stops before 4, giving 1, 2, 3.","code":"for n in range(1, 4):\n    print(\"Step\", n)\n\ntotal = 0\nwhile total < 10:\n    total += 4\nprint(total)","output":"Step 1\nStep 2\nStep 3\n12","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"`1...3` includes the 3. `1..<3` doesn't.","code":"for n in 1...3 {\n    print(\"Step\", n)\n}\n\nvar total = 0\nwhile total < 10 {\n    total += 4\n}\nprint(total)","output":"Step 1\nStep 2\nStep 3\n12","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"The classic three-part `for`: start, keep-going check, step.","code":"public class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        for (int n = 1; n <= 3; n++) {\n            System.out.println(\"Step \" + n);\n        }\n\n        int total = 0;\n        while (total < 10) {\n            total += 4;\n        }\n        System.out.println(total);\n    }\n}","output":"Step 1\nStep 2\nStep 3\n12","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","note":"`PERFORM` is the only loop. `VARYING` counts, `UNTIL` waits.","code":"       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.\n       PROGRAM-ID. LOOPS.\n       DATA DIVISION.\n       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.\n       01  WS-N      PIC 9(2)  VALUE 0.\n       01  WS-TOTAL  PIC 9(2)  VALUE 0.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           PERFORM VARYING WS-N FROM 1 BY 1 UNTIL WS-N > 3\n               DISPLAY \"Step \" WS-N\n           END-PERFORM.\n           PERFORM UNTIL WS-TOTAL >= 10\n               ADD 4 TO WS-TOTAL\n           END-PERFORM.\n           DISPLAY WS-TOTAL.\n           STOP RUN.","output":"Step 01\nStep 02\nStep 03\n12","checked":"ran"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"`for...of` is the clean way to walk through a list.","code":"for (let n = 1; n <= 3; n++) {\n  console.log(\"Step\", n);\n}\n\nlet total = 0;\nwhile (total < 10) {\n  total += 4;\n}\nconsole.log(total);","output":"Step 1\nStep 2\nStep 3\n12","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"full","note":"Same as JavaScript. The loop counter's type is worked out for you.","code":"for (let n = 1; n <= 3; n++) {\n  console.log(\"Step\", n);\n}\n\nlet total: number = 0;\nwhile (total < 10) {\n  total += 4;\n}\nconsole.log(total);","output":"Step 1\nStep 2\nStep 3\n12","checked":"ran"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"full","skip":"HTML has no loops. To repeat something on a page, JavaScript builds it."},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"full","skip":"CSS has no loops. One rule already applies to every match."}},"title":"Repeating","group":"Basics","blurb":"Do the same thing again without writing it twice.","explain":"A loop runs a block more than once. There are two kinds you'll use all the time.\n\nOne goes through a known set of steps: \"for each of these\". The other keeps going while something is true: \"until the total reaches 10\". Pick the first when you know how many times, and the second when you don't.","mainframe":"The classic mainframe loop reads a file: `PERFORM UNTIL END-OF-FILE`, one record at a time. Watch the field size. With `PIC 9`, a counter can't go past 9, so `UNTIL WS-N > 9` never comes true and the job loops until the operator cancels it."},{"id":"lists","docs":["array","index"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"`fruits[-1]` also works: it's the last item.","code":"fruits = [\"apple\", \"pear\", \"plum\"]\nfruits.append(\"fig\")\n\nprint(fruits[0])\nprint(len(fruits))\nfor fruit in fruits:\n    print(fruit)","output":"apple\n4\napple\npear\nplum\nfig","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"`var` makes the list changeable. With `let`, `append` won't compile.","code":"var fruits = [\"apple\", \"pear\", \"plum\"]\nfruits.append(\"fig\")\n\nprint(fruits[0])\nprint(fruits.count)\nfor fruit in fruits {\n    print(fruit)\n}","output":"apple\n4\napple\npear\nplum\nfig","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"Plain arrays can't grow, so most code uses `ArrayList`.","code":"import java.util.ArrayList;\nimport java.util.List;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<String> fruits = new ArrayList<>(List.of(\"apple\", \"pear\", \"plum\"));\n        fruits.add(\"fig\");\n\n        System.out.println(fruits.get(0));\n        System.out.println(fruits.size());\n        for (String fruit : fruits) {\n            System.out.println(fruit);\n        }\n    }\n}","output":"apple\n4\napple\npear\nplum\nfig","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","note":"Fixed size, counted from 1, and you track how many are in use.","code":"       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.\n       PROGRAM-ID. LISTS.\n       DATA DIVISION.\n       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.\n       01  WS-FRUITS.\n           05  WS-FRUIT  PIC X(10)  OCCURS 4 TIMES.\n       01  WS-COUNT      PIC 9      VALUE 3.\n       01  WS-I          PIC 9.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           MOVE \"apple\" TO WS-FRUIT(1).\n           MOVE \"pear\"  TO WS-FRUIT(2).\n           MOVE \"plum\"  TO WS-FRUIT(3).\n           ADD 1 TO WS-COUNT.\n           MOVE \"fig\"   TO WS-FRUIT(WS-COUNT).\n\n           DISPLAY WS-FRUIT(1).\n           DISPLAY WS-COUNT.\n           PERFORM VARYING WS-I FROM 1 BY 1 UNTIL WS-I > WS-COUNT\n               DISPLAY WS-FRUIT(WS-I)\n           END-PERFORM.\n           STOP RUN.","output":"apple\n4\napple\npear\nplum\nfig","checked":"ran"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"`length` is a value, not a function: no brackets.","code":"const fruits = [\"apple\", \"pear\", \"plum\"];\nfruits.push(\"fig\");\n\nconsole.log(fruits[0]);\nconsole.log(fruits.length);\nfor (const fruit of fruits) {\n  console.log(fruit);\n}","output":"apple\n4\napple\npear\nplum\nfig","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"full","note":"`string[]` means every item must be text.","code":"const fruits: string[] = [\"apple\", \"pear\", \"plum\"];\nfruits.push(\"fig\");\n\nconsole.log(fruits[0]);\nconsole.log(fruits.length);\nfor (const fruit of fruits) {\n  console.log(fruit);\n}","output":"apple\n4\napple\npear\nplum\nfig","checked":"ran"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"live","note":"A list on the page: `ul` for bullets, `ol` for numbers.","code":"<ul>\n  <li>apple</li>\n  <li>pear</li>\n  <li>plum</li>\n  <li>fig</li>\n</ul>","checked":"live"},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"full","skip":"CSS can style a list, but it can't hold one."}},"title":"Lists","group":"Basics","blurb":"Keep many values in order under one name.","explain":"A list, also called an array, holds values in order. You reach into it by position, called an index.\n\nNearly every language counts from 0, so the first item is `[0]`. Lists can usually grow as you add things.","mainframe":"A COBOL table (`OCCURS`) is a fixed number of slots set at compile time, and it counts from 1. Using slot 0, or one past the end, overwrites whatever is next to it in memory. Real shops compile with `SSRANGE` on during testing to catch that. You keep your own count of how many slots are in use."},{"id":"maps","docs":["map","hash-table"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"`.get` gives a fallback. `stock[\"kiwi\"]` would crash.","code":"stock = {\"apple\": 5, \"pear\": 0}\nstock[\"plum\"] = 12\n\nprint(stock[\"apple\"])\nprint(stock.get(\"kiwi\", 0))\nfor name, count in stock.items():\n    print(name, count)","output":"5\n0\napple 5\npear 0\nplum 12","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"Dictionaries have no fixed order, so this sorts before printing.","code":"var stock = [\"apple\": 5, \"pear\": 0]\nstock[\"plum\"] = 12\n\nprint(stock[\"apple\"] ?? 0)\nprint(stock[\"kiwi\"] ?? 0)\nfor (name, count) in stock.sorted(by: { $0.key < $1.key }) {\n    print(name, count)\n}","output":"5\n0\napple 5\npear 0\nplum 12","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"`LinkedHashMap` keeps the order you added things.","code":"import java.util.LinkedHashMap;\nimport java.util.Map;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Map<String, Integer> stock = new LinkedHashMap<>();\n        stock.put(\"apple\", 5);\n        stock.put(\"pear\", 0);\n        stock.put(\"plum\", 12);\n\n        System.out.println(stock.get(\"apple\"));\n        System.out.println(stock.getOrDefault(\"kiwi\", 0));\n        stock.forEach((name, count) -> System.out.println(name + \" \" + count));\n    }\n}","output":"5\n0\napple 5\npear 0\nplum 12","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","note":"Walk the table with `SEARCH`. `IX` is an index, a fast position marker.","code":"       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.\n       PROGRAM-ID. LOOKUP.\n       DATA DIVISION.\n       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.\n       01  WS-STOCK.\n           05  WS-ITEM   OCCURS 3 TIMES INDEXED BY IX.\n               10  WS-KEY    PIC X(8).\n               10  WS-QTY    PIC 9(3).\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           MOVE \"apple\" TO WS-KEY(1).\n           MOVE 5       TO WS-QTY(1).\n           MOVE \"pear\"  TO WS-KEY(2).\n           MOVE 0       TO WS-QTY(2).\n           MOVE \"plum\"  TO WS-KEY(3).\n           MOVE 12      TO WS-QTY(3).\n\n           SET IX TO 1.\n           SEARCH WS-ITEM\n               AT END DISPLAY \"none\"\n               WHEN WS-KEY(IX) = \"plum\"\n                   DISPLAY WS-QTY(IX)\n           END-SEARCH.\n           STOP RUN.","output":"012","checked":"ran"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"A plain object works for simple data. `??` gives a fallback.","code":"const stock = { apple: 5, pear: 0 };\nstock.plum = 12;\n\nconsole.log(stock.apple);\nconsole.log(stock.kiwi ?? 0);\nfor (const [name, count] of Object.entries(stock)) {\n  console.log(name, count);\n}","output":"5\n0\napple 5\npear 0\nplum 12","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"full","note":"`Record<string, number>` says: text keys, number values.","code":"const stock: Record<string, number> = { apple: 5, pear: 0 };\nstock.plum = 12;\n\nconsole.log(stock.apple);\nconsole.log(stock.kiwi ?? 0);\nfor (const [name, count] of Object.entries(stock)) {\n  console.log(name, count);\n}","output":"5\n0\napple 5\npear 0\nplum 12","checked":"ran"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"full","skip":"HTML has no lookups, though `<dl>` can show word-and-meaning pairs."},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"full","skip":"CSS can't look values up."}},"title":"Looking things up","group":"Basics","blurb":"Find a value by its name instead of its position.","explain":"A map stores pairs: a key and its value, like a word and its meaning in a dictionary. You ask for the key and get the value straight away, without searching.\n\nDifferent languages call it a dictionary, a map, or an object. Always decide what happens when the key isn't there.","mainframe":"COBOL has no built-in map. For a small table in memory you use `SEARCH`, or `SEARCH ALL` for a fast binary search on a sorted table. For real data the mainframe answer is a VSAM KSDS file: you read a record straight by its key, with billions of records."},{"id":"functions","docs":["function","parameter","return-value"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"`rate=0.2` is a default, used when you leave it out.","code":"def add_tax(price, rate=0.2):\n    return price * (1 + rate)\n\nprint(add_tax(100))\nprint(add_tax(100, 0.05))","output":"120.0\n105.0","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"The label `rate:` is part of the call. `_` means no label.","code":"func addTax(_ price: Double, rate: Double = 0.2) -> Double {\n    return price * (1 + rate)\n}\n\nprint(addTax(100))\nprint(addTax(100, rate: 0.05))","output":"120.0\n105.0","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"No default values. You write a second method with the same name instead.","code":"public class Main {\n    static double addTax(double price, double rate) {\n        return price * (1 + rate);\n    }\n\n    static double addTax(double price) {\n        return addTax(price, 0.2);\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(addTax(100));\n        System.out.println(addTax(100, 0.05));\n    }\n}","output":"120.0\n105.0","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","note":"`PERFORM` runs a paragraph, and values are shared, not passed.","code":"       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.\n       PROGRAM-ID. TAXES.\n       DATA DIVISION.\n       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.\n       01  WS-PRICE   PIC 9(5)V99  VALUE 100.\n       01  WS-RATE    PIC 9V99     VALUE 0.20.\n       01  WS-RESULT  PIC 9(5)V99.\n       01  WS-OUT     PIC ZZ,ZZ9.99.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n       MAIN-PARA.\n           PERFORM ADD-TAX.\n           MOVE 0.05 TO WS-RATE.\n           PERFORM ADD-TAX.\n           STOP RUN.\n       ADD-TAX.\n           COMPUTE WS-RESULT = WS-PRICE * (1 + WS-RATE).\n           MOVE WS-RESULT TO WS-OUT.\n           DISPLAY WS-OUT.","output":"   120.00\n   105.00","checked":"ran"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"Arrow functions, `(a) => ...`, are a shorter way to write the same thing.","code":"function addTax(price, rate = 0.2) {\n  return price * (1 + rate);\n}\n\nconsole.log(addTax(100));\nconsole.log(addTax(100, 0.05));","output":"120\n105","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"full","note":"Types on the way in and the way out. A wrong call won't compile.","code":"function addTax(price: number, rate: number = 0.2): number {\n  return price * (1 + rate);\n}\n\nconsole.log(addTax(100));\nconsole.log(addTax(100, 0.05));","output":"120\n105","checked":"ran"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"full","skip":"HTML has no functions. Web components come close, but they need JavaScript."},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"live","note":"CSS has built-in functions like `calc()`, and `:is()` can reuse a rule.","demo":"<h1>Title</h1><h2>Subtitle</h2>","code":":is(h1, h2) {\n  color: seagreen;\n  font-family: Georgia, serif;\n}","checked":"live"}},"title":"Functions","group":"Basics","blurb":"Name a piece of work, then reuse it anywhere.","explain":"A function is a small named machine. Values go in as parameters, it does its work, and a value comes back.\n\nIt stops you repeating yourself, and gives a job a name, so the code that uses it reads clearly.","mainframe":"A paragraph is a named block of code that you run with `PERFORM`. It takes no parameters and returns nothing: every paragraph shares the same Working-Storage, which is why you set `WS-RATE` first. For real parameters you write a separate program and `CALL` it with `USING`, and it receives them in its `LINKAGE SECTION`."},{"id":"records","docs":["class","object","struct","group-item"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"`@dataclass` writes the boring setup code for you.","code":"from dataclasses import dataclass\n\n@dataclass\nclass Customer:\n    name: str\n    balance: float\n\n    def deposit(self, amount):\n        self.balance += amount\n\nc = Customer(\"Asha\", 100.0)\nc.deposit(50.0)\nprint(c.name, c.balance)","output":"Asha 150.0","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"A `struct` is copied when passed around, so changing it needs `mutating`.","code":"struct Customer {\n    let name: String\n    var balance: Double\n\n    mutating func deposit(_ amount: Double) {\n        balance += amount\n    }\n}\n\nvar c = Customer(name: \"Asha\", balance: 100)\nc.deposit(50)\nprint(c.name, c.balance)","output":"Asha 150.0","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"Fields, a constructor, then methods. Java likes to spell things out.","code":"public class Main {\n    static class Customer {\n        final String name;\n        double balance;\n\n        Customer(String name, double balance) {\n            this.name = name;\n            this.balance = balance;\n        }\n\n        void deposit(double amount) {\n            balance += amount;\n        }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Customer c = new Customer(\"Asha\", 100.0);\n        c.deposit(50.0);\n        System.out.println(c.name + \" \" + c.balance);\n    }\n}","output":"Asha 150.0","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","note":"`01` is the record, `05` the fields inside. It's a map of bytes.","code":"       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.\n       PROGRAM-ID. RECORDS1.\n       DATA DIVISION.\n       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.\n       01  WS-CUSTOMER.\n           05  CUST-NAME     PIC X(10)    VALUE \"Asha\".\n           05  CUST-BALANCE  PIC 9(5)V99  VALUE 100.00.\n       01  WS-AMOUNT         PIC 9(5)V99  VALUE 50.00.\n       01  WS-OUT            PIC ZZ,ZZ9.99.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           ADD WS-AMOUNT TO CUST-BALANCE.\n           MOVE CUST-BALANCE TO WS-OUT.\n           DISPLAY CUST-NAME WS-OUT.\n           STOP RUN.","output":"Asha         150.00","checked":"ran"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"Or skip the class and write a plain object: `{ name: \"Asha\", balance: 100 }`.","code":"class Customer {\n  constructor(name, balance) {\n    this.name = name;\n    this.balance = balance;\n  }\n\n  deposit(amount) {\n    this.balance += amount;\n  }\n}\n\nconst c = new Customer(\"Asha\", 100);\nc.deposit(50);\nconsole.log(c.name, c.balance);","output":"Asha 150","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"full","note":"`interface` describes a shape, and any object of that shape fits.","code":"interface Customer {\n  name: string;\n  balance: number;\n}\n\nfunction deposit(customer: Customer, amount: number): Customer {\n  return { ...customer, balance: customer.balance + amount };\n}\n\nconst c = deposit({ name: \"Asha\", balance: 100 }, 50);\nconsole.log(c.name, c.balance);","output":"Asha 150","checked":"ran"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"live","note":"HTML groups by nesting. `class` is a label that CSS and JavaScript can find.","code":"<article class=\"customer\">\n  <h2>Asha</h2>\n  <p>Balance: 150</p>\n</article>","checked":"live"},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"live","note":"A class name groups elements so they share a style.","demo":"<article class=\"customer\"><h2>Asha</h2><p>Balance: 150</p></article>","code":".customer {\n  padding: 12px;\n  border: 1px solid #ccc;\n  border-radius: 8px;\n}","checked":"live"}},"title":"Grouping data","group":"Structure","blurb":"Keep related values together as one thing.","explain":"A customer has a name and a balance. Keeping them in two loose variables falls apart fast, so you group them into one thing.\n\nModern languages give you a class or struct: a template for data, plus functions that belong to it, called methods.","mainframe":"A COBOL record is a layout of bytes, written with level numbers. `01` is the whole record, and `05` is a field inside it. There are no methods: the data sits in one place and paragraphs act on it. That layout is usually kept in a copybook and shared by every program touching the file. `REDEFINES` lets the same bytes be read two different ways."},{"id":"missing","docs":["null","optional"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"`None` is the nothing value. Test it with `is None`.","code":"nicknames = {\"asha\": \"Ash\"}\nnickname = nicknames.get(\"ravi\")\n\nif nickname is None:\n    print(\"No nickname\")\nelse:\n    print(nickname.upper())","output":"No nickname","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"`String?` might be nil. `if let` opens it safely, `??` gives a fallback.","code":"let nicknames = [\"asha\": \"Ash\"]\nlet nickname: String? = nicknames[\"ravi\"]\n\nif let nick = nickname {\n    print(nick.uppercased())\n} else {\n    print(\"No nickname\")\n}\nprint(nickname ?? \"none\")","output":"No nickname\nnone","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"`Optional` wraps a maybe-missing value, so you can't forget to check.","code":"import java.util.Map;\nimport java.util.Optional;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Map<String, String> nicknames = Map.of(\"asha\", \"Ash\");\n        Optional<String> nickname = Optional.ofNullable(nicknames.get(\"ravi\"));\n\n        System.out.println(nickname.map(String::toUpperCase).orElse(\"No nickname\"));\n    }\n}","output":"No nickname","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","note":"No null here. An 88 level gives a name to \"the field is blank\".","code":"       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.\n       PROGRAM-ID. MISSING1.\n       DATA DIVISION.\n       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.\n       01  WS-NICK        PIC X(10)  VALUE SPACES.\n           88  NO-NICK               VALUE SPACES.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           IF NO-NICK\n               DISPLAY \"No nickname\"\n           ELSE\n               DISPLAY WS-NICK\n           END-IF.\n           STOP RUN.","output":"No nickname","checked":"ran"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"There are two nothings: `undefined` and `null`. `?.` stops at either one.","code":"const nicknames = { asha: \"Ash\" };\nconst nickname = nicknames[\"ravi\"];\n\nconsole.log(nickname);\nconsole.log(nickname?.toUpperCase() ?? \"No nickname\");","output":"undefined\nNo nickname","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"full","note":"`string | undefined` forces the check. Skip it and this won't compile.","code":"const nicknames: Record<string, string | undefined> = { asha: \"Ash\" };\nconst nickname = nicknames[\"ravi\"];\n\nif (nickname === undefined) {\n  console.log(\"No nickname\");\n} else {\n  console.log(nickname.toUpperCase());\n}","output":"No nickname","checked":"ran"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"full","skip":"Nothing to handle: an empty element just shows nothing."},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"live","note":"`:empty` can style an element with nothing in it.","demo":"<p class=\"note\"></p><p class=\"note\">I have text.</p>","code":".note {\n  padding: 8px;\n  background: #fde68a;\n}\n\n.note:empty {\n  display: none;\n}","checked":"live"}},"title":"When there's nothing there","group":"Structure","blurb":"Handle a value that might not exist.","explain":"Sometimes the answer is \"nothing\": no such customer, no nickname, no reply. Most languages have a value for that, called `null`, `nil` or `None`. Using it as if it were real is one of the most common crashes in programming.\n\nSwift and TypeScript make this safer: the type says a value might be missing, and you have to deal with it before the code will compile.","mainframe":"COBOL has no null. A field always holds bytes, so you agree on a meaning for \"nothing\": usually `SPACES` for text, zero for numbers, or `LOW-VALUES`. An 88 level names that meaning. Db2 does have NULL, so programs pair a field with an indicator variable, and a negative indicator means null."},{"id":"errors","docs":["exception","abend"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"Name the error you expect. A bare `except` hides real bugs.","code":"def safe_divide(a, b):\n    try:\n        return a / b\n    except ZeroDivisionError:\n        print(\"Can't divide by zero\")\n        return None\n\nprint(safe_divide(10, 2))\nprint(safe_divide(10, 0))","output":"5.0\nCan't divide by zero\nNone","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"A function marked `throws` must be called with `try`.","code":"enum MathError: Error {\n    case divideByZero\n}\n\nfunc divide(_ a: Int, by b: Int) throws -> Int {\n    guard b != 0 else { throw MathError.divideByZero }\n    return a / b\n}\n\ndo {\n    print(try divide(10, by: 2))\n    print(try divide(10, by: 0))\n} catch {\n    print(\"Can't divide by zero\")\n}","output":"5\nCan't divide by zero","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"Some exceptions must be caught, or the code won't compile.","code":"public class Main {\n    static int divide(int a, int b) {\n        return a / b;\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        try {\n            System.out.println(divide(10, 2));\n            System.out.println(divide(10, 0));\n        } catch (ArithmeticException e) {\n            System.out.println(\"Can't divide by zero\");\n        }\n    }\n}","output":"5\nCan't divide by zero","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","note":"Check right at the statement that could fail.","code":"       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.\n       PROGRAM-ID. ERRORS1.\n       DATA DIVISION.\n       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.\n       01  WS-A      PIC 9(3)  VALUE 10.\n       01  WS-B      PIC 9(3)  VALUE 0.\n       01  WS-R      PIC 9(3)  VALUE 0.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           DIVIDE WS-A BY WS-B GIVING WS-R\n               ON SIZE ERROR\n                   DISPLAY \"Can't divide by zero\"\n               NOT ON SIZE ERROR\n                   DISPLAY WS-R\n           END-DIVIDE.\n           STOP RUN.","output":"Can't divide by zero","checked":"ran"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"Dividing by zero gives `Infinity`, with no error. Bad JSON does throw.","code":"console.log(10 / 0);\n\ntry {\n  JSON.parse(\"not json\");\n} catch (err) {\n  console.log(\"Bad JSON:\", err.name);\n}","output":"Infinity\nBad JSON: SyntaxError","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"full","note":"In a `catch`, the error's type is `unknown`, so you check it first.","code":"console.log(10 / 0);\n\ntry {\n  JSON.parse(\"not json\");\n} catch (err) {\n  if (err instanceof Error) {\n    console.log(\"Bad JSON:\", err.name);\n  }\n}","output":"Infinity\nBad JSON: SyntaxError","checked":"ran"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"live","note":"Browsers forgive bad HTML and keep going. `alt` covers a failed image.","code":"<img src=\"missing.png\" alt=\"A photo of the shop\">","checked":"live"},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"live","note":"A browser skips rules it doesn't understand, so fallbacks go first.","demo":"<p class=\"box\">The second colour wins if the browser knows it.</p>","code":".box {\n  background: #fde68a;\n  background: color-mix(in srgb, gold, white 60%);\n  padding: 8px;\n}","checked":"live"}},"title":"When things go wrong","group":"Structure","blurb":"Catch problems instead of crashing.","explain":"Files go missing, networks drop, people type letters where numbers should go. Good programs expect that.\n\nThe usual pattern is `try` and `catch`: try the risky thing, and if it throws an error, catch it and decide what to do. Notice that JavaScript doesn't treat dividing by zero as an error at all.","mainframe":"COBOL doesn't throw exceptions. You check after each risky step: `ON SIZE ERROR` for arithmetic, `FILE STATUS` for files, `SQLCODE` for Db2. If nobody checks, the job can ABEND (abnormal end). S0C7 means bad data in a number field, and it's the most famous one. Jobs also leave a return code: 0 is fine, 4 is a warning, and 8 or more means trouble."},{"id":"files","docs":["file","dataset"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"`with` closes the file for you, even after an error.","code":"with open(\"names.txt\") as f:\n    for line in f:\n        print(line.strip().upper())","output":"ASHA\nRAVI\nMEI","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"Reads the whole file at once, then splits it into lines.","code":"import Foundation\n\nlet text = try String(contentsOfFile: \"names.txt\", encoding: .utf8)\nfor line in text.split(separator: \"\\n\") {\n    print(line.uppercased())\n}","output":"ASHA\nRAVI\nMEI","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"`throws Exception` passes file errors up, so you don't handle them here.","code":"import java.nio.file.Files;\nimport java.nio.file.Path;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        for (String line : Files.readAllLines(Path.of(\"names.txt\"))) {\n            System.out.println(line.toUpperCase());\n        }\n    }\n}","output":"ASHA\nRAVI\nMEI","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","note":"The pattern at the heart of mainframe batch work.","code":"       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.\n       PROGRAM-ID. READFILE.\n       ENVIRONMENT DIVISION.\n       INPUT-OUTPUT SECTION.\n       FILE-CONTROL.\n           SELECT NAMES-FILE ASSIGN TO NAMES\n               ORGANIZATION IS LINE SEQUENTIAL\n               FILE STATUS IS WS-STATUS.\n       DATA DIVISION.\n       FILE SECTION.\n       FD  NAMES-FILE.\n       01  NAME-RECORD      PIC X(20).\n       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.\n       01  WS-STATUS        PIC XX.\n       01  WS-EOF           PIC X   VALUE \"N\".\n           88  END-OF-FILE          VALUE \"Y\".\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           OPEN INPUT NAMES-FILE.\n           PERFORM UNTIL END-OF-FILE\n               READ NAMES-FILE\n                   AT END SET END-OF-FILE TO TRUE\n                   NOT AT END\n                       DISPLAY FUNCTION UPPER-CASE(NAME-RECORD)\n               END-READ\n           END-PERFORM.\n           CLOSE NAMES-FILE.\n           STOP RUN.","output":"ASHA\nRAVI\nMEI","checked":"ran"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"This is Node. A web page can't read your disk unless you pick a file.","play":"no","code":"const fs = require(\"node:fs\");\n\nconst lines = fs.readFileSync(\"names.txt\", \"utf8\").trim().split(\"\\n\");\nfor (const line of lines) {\n  console.log(line.toUpperCase());\n}","output":"ASHA\nRAVI\nMEI","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"full","note":"`import` is the modern way to bring in Node's file tools.","code":"import { readFileSync } from \"node:fs\";\n\nconst lines: string[] = readFileSync(\"names.txt\", \"utf8\").trim().split(\"\\n\");\nfor (const line of lines) {\n  console.log(line.toUpperCase());\n}","output":"ASHA\nRAVI\nMEI","checked":"ran"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"live","note":"A web page only gets a file when the person picks it.","code":"<label>\n  Pick a file:\n  <input type=\"file\" accept=\".txt\">\n</label>","checked":"live"},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"full","skip":"CSS doesn't read files, though it can load fonts and images."},"jcl":{"lang":"jcl","run":"none","note":"How the mainframe connects `ASSIGN TO NAMES` to a real dataset.","why":"JCL needs a mainframe to run","code":"//READJOB  JOB (ACCT),'READ NAMES',CLASS=A,MSGCLASS=X\n//STEP1    EXEC PGM=READFILE\n//STEPLIB  DD DSN=PROD.LOADLIB,DISP=SHR\n//NAMES    DD DSN=PROD.CUST.NAMES,DISP=SHR\n//SYSOUT   DD SYSOUT=*","checked":"none"}},"title":"Reading a file","group":"Structure","blurb":"Go through a file one line at a time.","explain":"Reading a file is the start of most real programs. You open it, go through it, and close it.\n\nThe tidy forms close the file for you, even if something fails. These all read `names.txt`, which holds three names.","mainframe":"This is what COBOL was made for. The program doesn't name a real file. `ASSIGN TO NAMES` is a nickname, and the JCL says what it means: `//NAMES DD DSN=PROD.CUST.NAMES,DISP=SHR`. So the same program can read test or production data with no code change. The pattern never changes: open, read until end of file, close. On z/OS you'd leave out `LINE SEQUENTIAL`, because mainframe records are fixed length with no line endings."},{"id":"sort","docs":["lambda","sort-step"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"`[x for x in list if ...]` is called a list comprehension.","code":"prices = [30, 5, 18, 42]\n\nprint(sorted(prices))\nprint([p for p in prices if p > 10])\nprint(sum(prices))","output":"[5, 18, 30, 42]\n[30, 18, 42]\n95","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"`$0` means \"the first thing passed in\".","code":"let prices = [30, 5, 18, 42]\n\nprint(prices.sorted())\nprint(prices.filter { $0 > 10 })\nprint(prices.reduce(0, +))","output":"[5, 18, 30, 42]\n[30, 18, 42]\n95","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"Streams chain steps together. `p -> p > 10` is a lambda.","code":"import java.util.List;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        List<Integer> prices = List.of(30, 5, 18, 42);\n\n        System.out.println(prices.stream().sorted().toList());\n        System.out.println(prices.stream().filter(p -> p > 10).toList());\n        System.out.println(prices.stream().mapToInt(Integer::intValue).sum());\n    }\n}","output":"[5, 18, 30, 42]\n[30, 18, 42]\n95","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","note":"Only GnuCOBOL and newer compilers sort a table. Most shops use a DFSORT step.","code":"       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.\n       PROGRAM-ID. SORTIT.\n       DATA DIVISION.\n       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.\n       01  WS-TABLE.\n           05  WS-PRICE  PIC 9(3)  OCCURS 4 TIMES.\n       01  WS-I          PIC 9.\n       01  WS-TOTAL      PIC 9(3)  VALUE 0.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           MOVE 30 TO WS-PRICE(1).\n           MOVE 5  TO WS-PRICE(2).\n           MOVE 18 TO WS-PRICE(3).\n           MOVE 42 TO WS-PRICE(4).\n           SORT WS-PRICE ASCENDING KEY WS-PRICE.\n           PERFORM VARYING WS-I FROM 1 BY 1 UNTIL WS-I > 4\n               ADD WS-PRICE(WS-I) TO WS-TOTAL\n               IF WS-PRICE(WS-I) > 10\n                   DISPLAY WS-PRICE(WS-I)\n               END-IF\n           END-PERFORM.\n           DISPLAY WS-TOTAL.\n           STOP RUN.","output":"018\n030\n042\n095","checked":"ran"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"Careful: plain `.sort()` compares as text, so 18 comes before 5.","code":"const prices = [30, 5, 18, 42];\n\nconsole.log([...prices].sort((a, b) => a - b).join(\", \"));\nconsole.log(prices.filter((p) => p > 10).join(\", \"));\nconsole.log(prices.reduce((sum, p) => sum + p, 0));","output":"5, 18, 30, 42\n30, 18, 42\n95","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"full","note":"Same as JavaScript, and `p` is known to be a number here.","code":"const prices: number[] = [30, 5, 18, 42];\n\nconsole.log([...prices].sort((a, b) => a - b).join(\", \"));\nconsole.log(prices.filter((p) => p > 10).join(\", \"));\nconsole.log(prices.reduce((sum, p) => sum + p, 0));","output":"5, 18, 30, 42\n30, 18, 42\n95","checked":"ran"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"full","skip":"HTML shows things in the order they're written."},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"live","note":"`order` moves things around on screen, without changing the HTML.","demo":"<div class=\"row\"><span>first</span><span class=\"last\">moved</span><span>third</span></div>","code":".row {\n  display: flex;\n  gap: 12px;\n}\n\n.last {\n  order: -1;\n  font-weight: bold;\n}","checked":"live"},"jcl":{"lang":"jcl","run":"none","note":"The usual mainframe way: no COBOL, just a sort step.","why":"JCL needs a mainframe to run","code":"//SORTSTEP EXEC PGM=SORT\n//SYSOUT   DD SYSOUT=*\n//SORTIN   DD DSN=PROD.PRICES.DAILY,DISP=SHR\n//SORTOUT  DD DSN=PROD.PRICES.SORTED,DISP=(NEW,CATLG),\n//            SPACE=(CYL,(5,5)),RECFM=FB,LRECL=80\n//SYSIN    DD *\n  SORT FIELDS=(1,3,CH,A)\n  INCLUDE COND=(1,3,CH,GT,C'010')\n/*","checked":"none"}},"title":"Sorting and filtering","group":"Structure","blurb":"Put things in order and keep only what you want.","explain":"Most work on data comes down to three moves: sort it, keep the bits you want, add it up.\n\nModern languages let you say what you want in one line, by passing a small function to `filter`. That's a lot easier to read than writing the loop yourself.","mainframe":"On a mainframe, you often don't sort in COBOL at all. You add a DFSORT step to the JCL, and it sorts, filters and even sums huge files with no code. Run it before your program, so the program only ever sees clean, sorted input. Batch logic usually depends on that sorted order."},{"id":"modules","docs":["library","package","copybook"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"Standard tools come with Python. Add others with `pip install`.","code":"import math\nfrom statistics import mean\n\nprint(math.sqrt(16))\nprint(mean([2, 4, 9]))","output":"4.0\n5","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"`Foundation` comes with Apple's tools. Others come via Swift Package Manager.","code":"import Foundation\n\nprint(sqrt(16.0))\nprint(floor(2.7))","output":"4.0\n2.0","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"`java.*` comes with Java. Libraries come through Maven or Gradle.","code":"import java.util.List;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        System.out.println(Math.sqrt(16));\n        System.out.println(List.of(2, 4, 9).size());\n    }\n}","output":"4.0\n3","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","note":"`CUSTREC.cpy` holds a record layout, shared by every program.","code":"       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.\n       PROGRAM-ID. USECOPY.\n       DATA DIVISION.\n       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.\n       COPY CUSTREC.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           MOVE \"Asha\" TO CUST-NAME.\n           DISPLAY CUST-NAME.\n           STOP RUN.","output":"Asha","checked":"ran"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"`node:` means built into Node. Others come through `npm install`.","play":"no","code":"import { basename } from \"node:path\";\n\nconsole.log(basename(\"/home/asha/report.txt\"));\nconsole.log(Math.sqrt(16));","output":"report.txt\n4","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"full","note":"Same as JavaScript. Many packages also ship their own types.","code":"import { basename } from \"node:path\";\n\nconsole.log(basename(\"/home/asha/report.txt\"));\nconsole.log(Math.sqrt(16));","output":"report.txt\n4","checked":"ran"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"live","note":"HTML pulls in the rest: `link` for styles, `script` for code.","code":"<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"styles.css\">\n<script src=\"app.js\" defer></script>","checked":"live"},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"live","note":"One stylesheet can load another.","code":"@import url(\"theme.css\");\n\nbody {\n  margin: 0;\n}","checked":"live"}},"title":"Using other people's code","group":"Structure","blurb":"Bring in code from another file or library.","explain":"Nobody writes everything from scratch. Each language has a way to pull in code from elsewhere: your own files, the built-in standard library, or packages other people have shared.\n\nThe word changes: `import`, `require`, `COPY`. The idea stays the same.","mainframe":"`COPY` pastes a copybook into your program when it compiles. It's plain text inclusion, and it's how every program agrees on a record layout. Change a copybook and all programs using it must be recompiled, or they'll read the bytes wrongly. Compiled programs live in a load library, and JCL's `STEPLIB` says where to find them."},{"id":"run","docs":["compiler","interpreter","jcl","jvm"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"none","hl":"bash","why":"This is a command, not a program","note":"Run directly, with no compile step you can see.","code":"python3 app.py","checked":"none"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"none","hl":"bash","why":"This is a command, not a program","note":"Run it as a script, or compile a program you can keep.","code":"swift app.swift\n\nswiftc app.swift -o app\n./app","checked":"none"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"none","hl":"bash","why":"This is a command, not a program","note":"Compiles to bytecode, and the JVM runs that.","code":"javac Main.java\njava Main","checked":"none"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"none","hl":"bash","why":"This is a command, not a program","note":"GnuCOBOL on your Mac. `-x` means make a program you can run.","code":"cobc -x app.cob\n./app","checked":"none"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"none","hl":"bash","why":"This is a command, not a program","note":"In Node, or load it in a web page with a script tag.","code":"node app.js","checked":"none"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"none","hl":"bash","why":"This is a command, not a program","note":"Browsers can't run TypeScript. `tsc` turns it into JavaScript first.","code":"npx tsc app.ts\nnode app.js","checked":"none"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"none","hl":"bash","why":"This is a command, not a program","note":"Nothing to compile. The browser reads it, so just open the file.","code":"open index.html","checked":"none"},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"full","skip":"CSS isn't run. It loads along with the HTML page."},"jcl":{"lang":"jcl","run":"none","note":"A real mainframe job: one step, with its files connected.","why":"JCL needs a mainframe to run","code":"//PAYROLL  JOB (ACCT),'NIGHTLY PAY',CLASS=A,MSGCLASS=X\n//STEP1    EXEC PGM=PAYCALC\n//STEPLIB  DD DSN=PROD.LOADLIB,DISP=SHR\n//EMPLOYEE DD DSN=PROD.EMPLOYEE.MASTER,DISP=SHR\n//REPORT   DD SYSOUT=*","checked":"none"}},"title":"Running your program","group":"Real work","blurb":"How the code you wrote actually gets to run.","explain":"Some languages are run line by line by an interpreter. Others are compiled first: translated into something the machine can run.\n\nHere's what you type for each one. On a mainframe you don't type a command at all: you submit a job, and it runs when its turn comes.","mainframe":"JCL is a set of instructions for the operating system: which program to run, and which datasets to give it. Each `EXEC` is a step, and each `DD` connects a file to the program. You submit the job, it waits in a queue, and you read the result in SDSF. Compiling is a job too. Production work runs overnight under a scheduler, and step return codes decide what runs next."},{"id":"database","docs":["database","sql","sql-injection","db2"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"`sqlite3` comes with Python. This one lives in memory and needs no setup.","code":"import sqlite3\n\ndb = sqlite3.connect(\":memory:\")\ndb.execute(\"CREATE TABLE customer (name TEXT, balance REAL)\")\ndb.execute(\"INSERT INTO customer VALUES (?, ?)\", (\"Asha\", 150.0))\n\nquery = \"SELECT name, balance FROM customer WHERE balance > ?\"\nfor row in db.execute(query, (100,)):\n    print(row)","output":"('Asha', 150.0)","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"none","note":"SwiftData saves your types, and writes the SQL for you.","why":"SwiftData's @Model needs full Xcode","code":"import SwiftData\n\n@Model\nclass Customer {\n    var name: String\n    var balance: Double\n\n    init(name: String, balance: Double) {\n        self.name = name\n        self.balance = balance\n    }\n}","checked":"none"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"compile","note":"JDBC is the standard way in. `?` keeps input safe.","code":"import java.sql.DriverManager;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {\n        String sql = \"SELECT name, balance FROM customer WHERE balance > ?\";\n        try (var db = DriverManager.getConnection(\"jdbc:postgresql://localhost/shop\");\n             var query = db.prepareStatement(sql)) {\n            query.setInt(1, 100);\n            var rows = query.executeQuery();\n            while (rows.next()) {\n                System.out.println(rows.getString(\"name\"));\n            }\n        }\n    }\n}","checked":"compiled"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"none","note":"This piece needs Db2's precompiler, so it can't run on your Mac.","why":"Needs Db2 and its precompiler","code":"           EXEC SQL\n               SELECT NAME, BALANCE\n                 INTO :WS-NAME, :WS-BALANCE\n                 FROM CUSTOMER\n                WHERE CUST_ID = :WS-ID\n           END-EXEC.\n           EVALUATE SQLCODE\n               WHEN 0\n                   DISPLAY WS-NAME\n               WHEN 100\n                   DISPLAY \"Not found\"\n               WHEN OTHER\n                   DISPLAY \"Db2 error \" SQLCODE\n           END-EVALUATE.","checked":"none"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"compile","note":"A web page never touches the database. It asks your server.","play":"no","code":"const res = await fetch(\"/api/customers?minBalance=100\");\nconst customers = await res.json();\n\nfor (const customer of customers) {\n  console.log(customer.name);\n}","checked":"compiled"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"compile","note":"A type describes what the server sends back.","code":"interface Customer {\n  name: string;\n  balance: number;\n}\n\nconst res = await fetch(\"/api/customers?minBalance=100\");\nconst customers = (await res.json()) as Customer[];\n\nfor (const customer of customers) {\n  console.log(customer.name);\n}","checked":"compiled"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"full","skip":"HTML can't reach a database. A server sends the data, and the page shows it."},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"full","skip":"CSS has nothing to do with data."}},"title":"Talking to a database","group":"Real work","blurb":"Save data that outlives your program, and ask questions of it.","explain":"A database keeps data safe and lets you ask for exactly what you need. SQL is the language nearly all of them speak, and it looks the same from every language below.\n\nOne rule matters everywhere: never paste a person's input into SQL. Use a `?` or `:name` placeholder, so input can only ever be data. Pasting it in is how SQL injection attacks happen.","mainframe":"In COBOL, SQL sits right inside the program between `EXEC SQL` and `END-EXEC`. A precompiler swaps it for real calls, and a BIND step prepares the plan. `:WS-NAME` is a host variable, your COBOL field. After every statement you check `SQLCODE`: 0 is fine, 100 means not found, and a negative number is an error."},{"id":"async","docs":["async","thread"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"`gather` runs them together and returns the answers in order.","code":"import asyncio\n\nasync def fetch_price(name, delay):\n    await asyncio.sleep(delay)\n    return f\"{name}: ok\"\n\nasync def main():\n    results = await asyncio.gather(\n        fetch_price(\"apple\", 0.2),\n        fetch_price(\"pear\", 0.1),\n    )\n    print(results)\n\nasyncio.run(main())","output":"['apple: ok', 'pear: ok']","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"`async let` starts the work. `await` collects it.","code":"func fetchPrice(_ name: String, delay: UInt64) async -> String {\n    try? await Task.sleep(nanoseconds: delay)\n    return \"\\(name): ok\"\n}\n\nasync let apple = fetchPrice(\"apple\", delay: 200_000_000)\nasync let pear = fetchPrice(\"pear\", delay: 100_000_000)\nprint(await [apple, pear])","output":"[\"apple: ok\", \"pear: ok\"]","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"`supplyAsync` runs work on another thread. `join` waits for the answer.","code":"import java.util.concurrent.CompletableFuture;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static String fetchPrice(String name, long delay) {\n        try {\n            Thread.sleep(delay);\n        } catch (InterruptedException e) {\n            throw new RuntimeException(e);\n        }\n        return name + \": ok\";\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        var apple = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> fetchPrice(\"apple\", 200));\n        var pear = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> fetchPrice(\"pear\", 100));\n        System.out.println(apple.join() + \", \" + pear.join());\n    }\n}","output":"apple: ok, pear: ok","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","skip":"COBOL runs one step at a time. Doing things at once is the scheduler's job."},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"JavaScript is built around this. `Promise.all` waits for every answer.","code":"const wait = (ms) => new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms));\n\nasync function fetchPrice(name, delay) {\n  await wait(delay);\n  return `${name}: ok`;\n}\n\nconst results = await Promise.all([\n  fetchPrice(\"apple\", 200),\n  fetchPrice(\"pear\", 100),\n]);\nconsole.log(results.join(\", \"));","output":"apple: ok, pear: ok","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"full","note":"`Promise<string>` means \"a string, later\".","code":"const wait = (ms: number): Promise<void> =>\n  new Promise((resolve) => setTimeout(resolve, ms));\n\nasync function fetchPrice(name: string, delay: number): Promise<string> {\n  await wait(delay);\n  return `${name}: ok`;\n}\n\nconst results = await Promise.all([\n  fetchPrice(\"apple\", 200),\n  fetchPrice(\"pear\", 100),\n]);\nconsole.log(results.join(\", \"));","output":"apple: ok, pear: ok","checked":"ran"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"live","note":"`defer` and `lazy` load things without holding up the page.","code":"<script src=\"app.js\" defer></script>\n<img src=\"photo.jpg\" alt=\"Shop front\" loading=\"lazy\">","checked":"live"},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"live","note":"CSS animates by itself, without blocking your JavaScript.","demo":"<div class=\"dot\"></div>","code":".dot {\n  width: 24px;\n  height: 24px;\n  border-radius: 50%;\n  background: tomato;\n  animation: pulse 1s infinite alternate;\n}\n\n@keyframes pulse {\n  to { transform: scale(1.6); }\n}","checked":"live"}},"title":"Doing several things at once","group":"Real work","blurb":"Start slow jobs together, instead of waiting for each in turn.","explain":"Fetching from the network is slow. If you wait for one thing, then the next, the whole app freezes up. Async code lets you start the work, get on with other things, and pick up the result when it's ready.\n\n`await` means \"pause this function until the answer is here\". These examples start two jobs at once, then collect both answers.","mainframe":"COBOL has no async. Batch programs run top to bottom on one track. Speed comes from outside the program: the scheduler runs jobs side by side, and big files are split so several jobs can each take a part. Online, CICS runs many transactions at once, each a separate short task. Parallel Sysplex and Db2 data sharing spread that over several machines."},{"id":"page","docs":["dom","swiftui","component","bms"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"compile","note":"`tkinter` comes with Python. For the web you'd use Django or Flask.","code":"import tkinter as tk\n\nwindow = tk.Tk()\nwindow.title(\"My shop\")\ntk.Label(window, text=\"My shop\", font=(\"Helvetica\", 24)).pack()\ntk.Label(window, text=\"Open today until 6pm.\").pack()\nwindow.mainloop()","checked":"compiled"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"compile","note":"SwiftUI describes the screen. Modifiers like `.padding()` adjust it.","code":"import SwiftUI\n\nstruct ShopView: View {\n    var body: some View {\n        VStack(alignment: .leading, spacing: 8) {\n            Text(\"My shop\").font(.largeTitle)\n            Text(\"Open today until 6pm.\")\n            Link(\"See the menu\", destination: URL(string: \"https://example.com\")!)\n        }\n        .padding()\n    }\n}","checked":"compiled"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"compile","note":"Swing is built into Java. Most Java screens today are web pages or Android.","code":"import javax.swing.JFrame;\nimport javax.swing.JLabel;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        JFrame window = new JFrame(\"My shop\");\n        window.add(new JLabel(\"Open today until 6pm.\"));\n        window.setSize(300, 120);\n        window.setDefaultCloseOperation(JFrame.EXIT_ON_CLOSE);\n        window.setVisible(true);\n    }\n}","checked":"compiled"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"none","note":"CICS sends a screen, then the program ends and waits for the next Enter.","why":"Needs CICS on a mainframe","code":"           MOVE \"OPEN TODAY UNTIL 6PM\" TO MSGO.\n           EXEC CICS SEND MAP('SHOPMAP') MAPSET('SHOPSET')\n                ERASE\n           END-EXEC.\n           EXEC CICS RETURN TRANSID('SHOP')\n                COMMAREA(WS-STATE)\n           END-EXEC.","checked":"none"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"compile","note":"JavaScript changes the page through the DOM, the browser's live copy of it.","code":"const title = document.createElement(\"h1\");\ntitle.textContent = \"My shop\";\ndocument.body.append(title);","checked":"compiled"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"compile","note":"The type says exactly which kind of element you've got.","code":"const title: HTMLHeadingElement = document.createElement(\"h1\");\ntitle.textContent = \"My shop\";\ndocument.body.append(title);","checked":"compiled"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"live","note":"Tags say what each thing is: a heading, a paragraph, a link.","code":"<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n  <head>\n    <title>My shop</title>\n  </head>\n  <body>\n    <h1>My shop</h1>\n    <p>Open today until <strong>6pm</strong>.</p>\n    <a href=\"#menu\">See the menu</a>\n  </body>\n</html>","checked":"live"},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"live","note":"CSS handles how it looks. The content stays in the HTML.","demo":"<h1>My shop</h1><p>Open today until <strong>6pm</strong>.</p><a href=\"#menu\">See the menu</a>","code":"body {\n  font-family: Georgia, serif;\n  max-width: 30rem;\n  margin: 2rem auto;\n}\n\nh1 {\n  color: seagreen;\n}","checked":"live"}},"title":"Putting things on screen","group":"Screens","blurb":"Show a title, some text and a link.","explain":"Each platform draws screens in its own way. The web describes a page in HTML, and the browser draws it. Apple's SwiftUI describes a view in Swift code. Older desktop tools build the window piece by piece.\n\nThe modern trend is the same everywhere: describe what should be on screen, and let the system work out how to draw it.","mainframe":"The classic mainframe screen is a 3270 terminal: 24 lines of 80 characters, usually green. A BMS map defines where the fields sit, and CICS sends it. A program doesn't sit waiting for you. It sends the screen and ends, and CICS starts it again when you press Enter. That's called pseudo-conversational, and it's how one machine serves thousands of users."},{"id":"layout","docs":["flexbox","box-model","responsive"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"compile","note":"`side=\"left\"` lines things up in a row.","code":"import tkinter as tk\n\nwindow = tk.Tk()\ntk.Label(window, text=\"Left\", bg=\"khaki\").pack(side=\"left\", padx=6)\ntk.Label(window, text=\"Right\", bg=\"khaki\").pack(side=\"left\", padx=6)\nwindow.mainloop()","checked":"compiled"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"compile","note":"`HStack` is a row. `.frame(maxWidth: .infinity)` shares the space.","code":"import SwiftUI\n\nstruct RowView: View {\n    var body: some View {\n        HStack(spacing: 12) {\n            Text(\"Left\")\n                .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)\n                .padding()\n                .background(.yellow, in: .rect(cornerRadius: 8))\n            Text(\"Right\")\n                .frame(maxWidth: .infinity)\n                .padding()\n                .background(.yellow, in: .rect(cornerRadius: 8))\n        }\n    }\n}","checked":"compiled"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"compile","note":"`GridLayout(1, 2, ...)` means one row of two cells.","code":"import java.awt.GridLayout;\nimport javax.swing.JFrame;\nimport javax.swing.JLabel;\nimport javax.swing.JPanel;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        JPanel row = new JPanel(new GridLayout(1, 2, 12, 0));\n        row.add(new JLabel(\"Left\"));\n        row.add(new JLabel(\"Right\"));\n\n        JFrame window = new JFrame();\n        window.add(row);\n        window.pack();\n        window.setVisible(true);\n    }\n}","checked":"compiled"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"none","hl":"text","note":"A BMS map. `POS` is line and column, and `ATTRB` sets the field's behaviour.","why":"BMS maps need CICS on a mainframe","code":"LEFTFLD  DFHMDF POS=(5,10),LENGTH=4,ATTRB=(ASKIP,BRT),INITIAL='LEFT'\nRIGHTFLD DFHMDF POS=(5,50),LENGTH=5,ATTRB=(ASKIP,BRT),INITIAL='RIGHT'","checked":"none"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"compile","note":"Add a class and let CSS do the work, rather than setting styles in JS.","code":"const row = document.querySelector(\".row\");\nrow.classList.add(\"is-wide\");","checked":"compiled"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"compile","note":"`querySelector` might find nothing, so TypeScript makes you check.","code":"const row = document.querySelector<HTMLDivElement>(\".row\");\nif (row) {\n  row.classList.add(\"is-wide\");\n}","checked":"compiled"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"live","note":"HTML only lists the boxes. Where they sit is CSS's job.","code":"<div class=\"row\">\n  <div class=\"box\">Left</div>\n  <div class=\"box\">Right</div>\n</div>","checked":"live"},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"live","note":"`display: flex` turns the children into a row. `flex: 1` shares the space.","demo":"<div class=\"row\"><div class=\"box\">Left</div><div class=\"box\">Right</div></div>","code":".row {\n  display: flex;\n  gap: 12px;\n}\n\n.box {\n  flex: 1;\n  padding: 16px;\n  border-radius: 8px;\n  background: #fde68a;\n}","checked":"live"}},"title":"Laying things out","group":"Screens","blurb":"Put two boxes side by side, with a gap and some colour.","explain":"Layout means saying where things go. Modern tools work in rows and columns: put these in a row, add a gap, share the space.\n\nOn the web that's CSS flexbox and grid. SwiftUI has `HStack` and `VStack`, which work almost the same way. Learn one and the other makes sense straight away.","mainframe":"There's no layout engine on a 3270 screen. Each field has an exact position, `POS=(line,column)`, in a grid of 24 by 80. That's what BMS maps describe, so a screen never moves or wraps. These are assembler macros, which is why they look so different from COBOL."},{"id":"click","docs":["event","state"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"compile","note":"`command=` gives the button a function to call.","code":"import tkinter as tk\n\nwindow = tk.Tk()\ncount = 0\n\ndef clicked():\n    global count\n    count += 1\n    button.config(text=f\"Clicked {count} times\")\n\nbutton = tk.Button(window, text=\"Clicked 0 times\", command=clicked)\nbutton.pack()\nwindow.mainloop()","checked":"compiled"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"none","note":"Change `@State` and SwiftUI redraws the view for you.","why":"SwiftUI's @State needs full Xcode","code":"import SwiftUI\n\nstruct CounterView: View {\n    @State private var count = 0\n\n    var body: some View {\n        Button(\"Clicked \\(count) times\") {\n            count += 1\n        }\n    }\n}","checked":"none"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"compile","note":"The lambda is the code that runs on each click.","code":"import javax.swing.JButton;\nimport javax.swing.JFrame;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static int count = 0;\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        JButton button = new JButton(\"Clicked 0 times\");\n        button.addActionListener(e -> {\n            count++;\n            button.setText(\"Clicked \" + count + \" times\");\n        });\n\n        JFrame window = new JFrame();\n        window.add(button);\n        window.pack();\n        window.setVisible(true);\n    }\n}","checked":"compiled"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"none","note":"A key press comes in as `EIBAID`. State lives in the COMMAREA.","why":"Needs CICS on a mainframe","code":"           EVALUATE EIBAID\n               WHEN DFHENTER\n                   ADD 1 TO WS-COUNT\n                   PERFORM SEND-SCREEN\n               WHEN DFHPF3\n                   EXEC CICS RETURN END-EXEC\n               WHEN OTHER\n                   PERFORM SEND-SCREEN\n           END-EVALUATE.","checked":"none"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"compile","note":"Find the element, then listen for its click.","demo":"<button id=\"btn\">Clicked 0 times</button>","code":"let count = 0;\nconst btn = document.querySelector(\"#btn\");\n\nbtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n  count += 1;\n  btn.textContent = `Clicked ${count} times`;\n});","checked":"compiled"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"compile","note":"The `!` tells TypeScript \"I know this exists\". If you're wrong, it crashes.","code":"let count: number = 0;\nconst btn = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>(\"#btn\")!;\n\nbtn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n  count += 1;\n  btn.textContent = `Clicked ${count} times`;\n});","checked":"compiled"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"live","note":"A script inside the page makes the button work. Try clicking it.","code":"<button id=\"btn\">Clicked 0 times</button>\n<script>\n  let count = 0;\n  const btn = document.querySelector(\"#btn\");\n  btn.addEventListener(\"click\", () => {\n    count += 1;\n    btn.textContent = `Clicked ${count} times`;\n  });\n</script>","checked":"live"},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"live","note":"CSS reacts without code: `:hover` and `:active` follow the mouse.","demo":"<button class=\"btn\">Press me</button>","code":".btn {\n  padding: 8px 16px;\n  border: 0;\n  border-radius: 6px;\n  background: seagreen;\n  color: white;\n}\n\n.btn:hover {\n  background: tomato;\n}\n\n.btn:active {\n  transform: scale(0.95);\n}","checked":"live"}},"title":"Reacting to a click","group":"Screens","blurb":"A button that counts how many times it's pressed.","explain":"Screens wait. Nothing happens until someone clicks, and then your code runs. A function that runs in reply is called an event handler.\n\nState is what the app remembers between clicks, such as the count. In SwiftUI and React you change the state, and the screen updates by itself. With plain JavaScript or Swing, you update the screen yourself.","mainframe":"A 3270 has no clicks. You fill in fields and press Enter or a PF key, and the whole screen goes back at once. `EIBAID` tells the program which key it was: PF3 usually means exit. Nothing stays in memory between screens, so state goes in the COMMAREA, which CICS hands to the next run."},{"id":"input","docs":["validation"],"langs":{"python":{"lang":"python","run":"full","note":"`input` always gives you text. Use `int()` to turn it into a number.","stdin":"Asha","code":"name = input(\"Your name? \")\nprint(f\"Hello, {name}!\")","output":"Your name? Hello, Asha!","checked":"ran"},"swift":{"lang":"swift","run":"full","note":"`readLine` might give nil, so `??` adds a fallback.","stdin":"Asha","code":"print(\"Your name?\")\nlet name = readLine() ?? \"friend\"\nprint(\"Hello, \\(name)!\")","output":"Your name?\nHello, Asha!","checked":"ran"},"java":{"lang":"java","run":"full","note":"`Scanner` reads what's typed, a line at a time.","stdin":"Asha","code":"import java.util.Scanner;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Scanner in = new Scanner(System.in);\n        System.out.println(\"Your name?\");\n        String name = in.nextLine();\n        System.out.println(\"Hello, \" + name + \"!\");\n    }\n}","output":"Your name?\nHello, Asha!","checked":"ran"},"cobol":{"lang":"cobol","run":"full","note":"In a batch job, `ACCEPT` reads a card from `SYSIN`, not a person.","stdin":"Asha","code":"       IDENTIFICATION DIVISION.\n       PROGRAM-ID. GREET.\n       DATA DIVISION.\n       WORKING-STORAGE SECTION.\n       01  WS-NAME   PIC X(10).\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           DISPLAY \"Your name?\".\n           ACCEPT WS-NAME.\n           DISPLAY \"Hello, \" FUNCTION TRIM(WS-NAME) \"!\".\n           STOP RUN.","output":"Your name?\nHello, Asha!","checked":"ran"},"js":{"lang":"js","run":"full","note":"In Node. On a web page you'd read a form's input instead.","play":"no","stdin":"Asha","code":"import { createInterface } from \"node:readline/promises\";\n\nconst rl = createInterface({ input: process.stdin, output: process.stdout });\nconst name = await rl.question(\"Your name? \");\nrl.close();\nconsole.log(`Hello, ${name}!`);","output":"Your name? Hello, Asha!","checked":"ran"},"ts":{"lang":"ts","run":"compile","note":"On a web page: read the input's `.value`. It's always text.","code":"const field = document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>(\"#name\")!;\nconst form = document.querySelector<HTMLFormElement>(\"form\")!;\n\nform.addEventListener(\"submit\", (event) => {\n  event.preventDefault();\n  alert(`Hello, ${field.value}!`);\n});","checked":"compiled"},"html":{"lang":"html","run":"live","note":"`required` and `type` give you free checks, with no code.","code":"<form>\n  <label>\n    Your name\n    <input name=\"name\" required>\n  </label>\n  <button>Say hello</button>\n</form>","checked":"live"},"css":{"lang":"css","run":"live","note":"`:invalid` styles a field until it's filled in properly.","demo":"<form><input required placeholder=\"Your name\"></form>","code":"input {\n  padding: 8px;\n  border: 2px solid seagreen;\n  border-radius: 6px;\n}\n\ninput:invalid {\n  border-color: tomato;\n}","checked":"live"},"jcl":{"lang":"jcl","run":"none","note":"Batch input: the cards under `SYSIN DD *` are what `ACCEPT` reads.","why":"JCL needs a mainframe to run","code":"//STEP1    EXEC PGM=GREET\n//SYSOUT   DD SYSOUT=*\n//SYSIN    DD *\nAsha\n/*","checked":"none"}},"title":"Asking for input","group":"Screens","blurb":"Get a name from a person, and use it.","explain":"Programs get interesting when people can tell them things. On a command line you read what someone types. On a web page you use a form.\n\nNever trust what you get. People make typos, and attackers type code on purpose. Check input before using it.","mainframe":"`ACCEPT` reads from SYSIN, and in batch nobody's typing: the input comes from cards in the JCL, under `//SYSIN DD *`. It's how you pass settings like a run date into a job. Online, input arrives as the fields of a BMS map through `EXEC CICS RECEIVE MAP`."}],"cards":[{"id":"cb-pic-pad","deck":"cobol","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print? (Mind the spaces.)","lang":"cobol","code":"       01  WS-ITEM  PIC X(5)  VALUE \"AB\".\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           DISPLAY \"[\" WS-ITEM \"]\".","answer":"[AB   ]","why":"`PIC X(5)` is always 5 characters, so it's padded with spaces on the right.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"cb-pic-zero","deck":"cobol","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"cobol","code":"       01  WS-COUNT  PIC 9(4)  VALUE 42.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           DISPLAY WS-COUNT.","answer":"0042","why":"`PIC 9(4)` is 4 digits, padded with zeros on the left.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"cb-truncate-text","deck":"cobol","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"cobol","code":"       01  WS-CODE  PIC X(3).\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           MOVE \"ABCDEF\" TO WS-CODE.\n           DISPLAY WS-CODE.","answer":"ABC","why":"A `MOVE` to a smaller text field cuts off the right side, with no warning.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"cb-truncate-number","deck":"cobol","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"cobol","code":"       01  WS-N  PIC 9(2).\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           MOVE 1234 TO WS-N.\n           DISPLAY WS-N.","answer":"34","why":"A `MOVE` to a smaller number field cuts off the left side, the big digits. Silent data loss, and a classic bug.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"cb-implied-decimal","deck":"cobol","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"cobol","code":"       01  WS-PRICE  PIC 9(3)V99  VALUE 12.50.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           DISPLAY WS-PRICE.","answer":"01250","why":"`V` is an invisible decimal point. The field stores 5 digits. You need an edited field like `ZZ9.99` to show the dot.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"cb-edited","deck":"cobol","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"cobol","code":"       01  WS-PRICE  PIC 9(3)V99  VALUE 12.50.\n       01  WS-OUT    PIC ZZ9.99.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           MOVE WS-PRICE TO WS-OUT.\n           DISPLAY \"[\" WS-OUT \"]\".","answer":"[ 12.50]","why":"`Z` turns a leading zero into a space, and the `.` is a real printed dot.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"cb-table-one","deck":"cobol","kind":"predict","ask":"Where do tables start? What does this print?","lang":"cobol","code":"       01  WS-T.\n           05  WS-CELL  PIC X  OCCURS 3 TIMES.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           MOVE \"A\" TO WS-CELL(1).\n           MOVE \"B\" TO WS-CELL(2).\n           MOVE \"C\" TO WS-CELL(3).\n           DISPLAY WS-CELL(2).","answer":"B","why":"COBOL tables start at 1, so `(2)` is the second item. In most languages it would be `[1]`.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"cb-88","deck":"cobol","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"cobol","code":"       01  WS-FLAG  PIC X  VALUE \"N\".\n           88  DONE        VALUE \"Y\".\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           SET DONE TO TRUE.\n           DISPLAY WS-FLAG.","answer":"Y","why":"`SET DONE TO TRUE` moves the 88's value into the field.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"cb-rounded","deck":"cobol","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"cobol","code":"       01  WS-A  PIC 9(2)V9  VALUE 1.5.\n       01  WS-B  PIC 9(2).\n       01  WS-C  PIC 9(2).\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           COMPUTE WS-B = WS-A.\n           COMPUTE WS-C ROUNDED = WS-A.\n           DISPLAY WS-B \" \" WS-C.","answer":"01 02","why":"Without `ROUNDED`, COBOL cuts off the extra digits. With it, 1.5 rounds up to 2.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"cb-perform-until","deck":"cobol","kind":"predict","ask":"How many times does the body run?","lang":"cobol","code":"       01  WS-N  PIC 9(2)  VALUE 5.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           PERFORM UNTIL WS-N >= 5\n               DISPLAY \"HI\"\n           END-PERFORM.\n           DISPLAY \"DONE\".","answer":"DONE","why":"`PERFORM UNTIL` checks before it starts. The answer is zero times. `WITH TEST AFTER` would run it once.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"cb-move-to","deck":"cobol","kind":"fill","ask":"Fill the gap to copy a value into a field.","lang":"cobol","code":"       01  WS-NAME  PIC X(10).\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           ____ \"ASHA\" TO WS-NAME.\n           DISPLAY WS-NAME.","answer":"MOVE","why":"`MOVE` is COBOL's assignment. It reads source first, then target.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"cb-end-if","deck":"cobol","kind":"fill","ask":"Fill the gap to close the `IF` properly.","lang":"cobol","code":"       01  WS-N  PIC 9  VALUE 1.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           IF WS-N = 1\n               DISPLAY \"ONE\"\n           ____.","answer":"END-IF","why":"Close with `END-IF`. Don't rely on a full stop, because it ends every open `IF` at once.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"cb-write-loop","deck":"cobol","kind":"write","ask":"Write a loop that shows the numbers 1 to 3 using `PERFORM VARYING`.","lang":"cobol","code":"       01  WS-I  PIC 9(2).","answerLang":"cobol","answer":"       01  WS-I  PIC 9(2).\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           PERFORM VARYING WS-I FROM 1 BY 1 UNTIL WS-I > 3\n               DISPLAY WS-I\n           END-PERFORM.","why":"`VARYING` sets the start, the step and the stop condition.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"cb-divisions","deck":"cobol","kind":"explain","ask":"Name the four divisions of a COBOL program, in order.","answer":"IDENTIFICATION (its name), ENVIRONMENT (files and machine), DATA (fields), PROCEDURE (the logic).","checked":"none"},{"id":"cb-columns","deck":"cobol","kind":"explain","ask":"In fixed-format COBOL, what are columns 7, 8 and 12, and why does 72 matter?","answer":"Column 7 marks comments with `*`. Area A starts at column 8, for divisions, paragraphs and 01 levels. Area B starts at column 12, for statements. Anything past column 72 is ignored.","checked":"none"},{"id":"cb-fix-loop","deck":"cobol","kind":"fix","ask":"This loop never stops. Fix it.","lang":"cobol","code":"       01  WS-N  PIC 9.\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           PERFORM VARYING WS-N FROM 1 BY 1 UNTIL WS-N > 9\n               DISPLAY WS-N\n           END-PERFORM.","answerLang":"cobol","answer":"       01  WS-N  PIC 9(2).\n       PROCEDURE DIVISION.\n           PERFORM VARYING WS-N FROM 1 BY 1 UNTIL WS-N > 9\n               DISPLAY WS-N\n           END-PERFORM.","why":"`PIC 9` holds 0 to 9. Adding 1 to 9 wraps to 0, so it never gets past 9. Give the counter room.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"fe-sort-trap","deck":"frontend","kind":"predict","ask":"A classic JavaScript trap. What does this print?","lang":"js","code":"console.log([10, 5, 1].sort().join(\" \"));","answer":"1 10 5","why":"By default `sort` compares as text, and \"10\" comes before \"5\". Pass `(a, b) => a - b` for numbers.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"fe-equality","deck":"frontend","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"js","code":"console.log(1 == \"1\");\nconsole.log(1 === \"1\");","answer":"true\nfalse","why":"`==` converts types first. `===` doesn't. Always use `===`.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"fe-plus","deck":"frontend","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"js","code":"console.log(\"5\" + 1);\nconsole.log(\"5\" - 1);","answer":"51\n4","why":"`+` with text joins. `-` can only do maths, so it converts the text to a number.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"fe-falsy","deck":"frontend","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"js","code":"const count = 0;\nconsole.log(count || 10);\nconsole.log(count ?? 10);","answer":"10\n0","why":"`||` falls back on anything falsy, including 0. `??` only falls back on null or undefined.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"fe-const-object","deck":"frontend","kind":"predict","ask":"Will this crash? What does it print?","lang":"js","code":"const user = { name: \"Asha\" };\nuser.name = \"Ravi\";\nconsole.log(user.name);","answer":"Ravi","why":"`const` fixes the variable, not the object. The object's insides can still change.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"fe-map","deck":"frontend","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"js","code":"console.log([1, 2, 3].map((n) => n * 2).join(\",\"));","answer":"2,4,6","why":"`map` makes a new list with each item changed.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"fe-await","deck":"frontend","kind":"fill","ask":"Fill the gap so `data` holds the result, not a promise.","lang":"js","code":"async function load() {\n  const res = ____ fetch(\"/api/items\");\n  return res.json();\n}","answer":"await","why":"`await` pauses the function until the promise finishes.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"fe-flex","deck":"frontend","kind":"fill","ask":"Fill the gap to line these children up in a row.","lang":"css","code":".row {\n  display: ____;\n  gap: 12px;\n}","answer":"flex","why":"`display: flex` puts direct children in a row.","checked":"none"},{"id":"fe-center","deck":"frontend","kind":"write","ask":"Write the CSS to centre a child in the middle of its parent, both ways.","lang":"css","code":".parent {\n  /* your CSS */\n}","answerLang":"css","answer":".parent {\n  display: flex;\n  justify-content: center;\n  align-items: center;\n}","why":"`justify-content` works along the row, and `align-items` across it. The parent needs some height for this to show.","checked":"none"},{"id":"fe-ts-union","deck":"frontend","kind":"write","ask":"Write a TypeScript type `Status` that can only be \"active\" or \"closed\".","lang":"ts","code":"const s: Status = \"active\";","answerLang":"ts","answer":"type Status = \"active\" | \"closed\";\nconst s: Status = \"active\";","why":"A union type limits a value to a set list, and typos are caught for you.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"fe-box-model","deck":"frontend","kind":"explain","ask":"What's the difference between padding and margin?","answer":"Padding is space inside the border. Margin is space outside it.","checked":"none"},{"id":"fe-semantic","deck":"frontend","kind":"explain","ask":"Why use `<button>` rather than a clickable `<div>`?","answer":"A real button works with the keyboard and screen readers, with no extra work.","checked":"none"},{"id":"fe-alt","deck":"frontend","kind":"explain","ask":"What's the `alt` attribute on an image for?","answer":"It describes the image for screen readers, and shows if the image can't load.","checked":"none"},{"id":"fe-specificity","deck":"frontend","kind":"explain","ask":"Two CSS rules target one element, and the wrong one wins. Why?","answer":"Specificity. An id beats a class, and a class beats a tag. When they tie, the later rule wins.","checked":"none"},{"id":"fe-dom","deck":"frontend","kind":"explain","ask":"What's the DOM?","answer":"The browser's live model of the page. JavaScript changes what you see by changing the DOM.","checked":"none"},{"id":"fe-ts-runtime","deck":"frontend","kind":"explain","ask":"Does TypeScript check types while the app is running?","answer":"No. Types are removed before it runs, so data from a server still needs checking.","checked":"none"},{"id":"fe-fix-event","deck":"frontend","kind":"fix","ask":"The form reloads the page on submit. Fix it.","lang":"js","code":"form.addEventListener(\"submit\", () => {\n  save();\n});","answerLang":"js","answer":"form.addEventListener(\"submit\", (event) => {\n  event.preventDefault();\n  save();\n});","why":"A form's default action reloads the page. `preventDefault` stops that.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"fe-fix-sum","deck":"frontend","kind":"fix","ask":"This should show 3. It shows \"12\". Fix it.","lang":"js","code":"const a = \"1\";\nconst b = \"2\";\nconsole.log(a + b);","answerLang":"js","answer":"const a = \"1\";\nconst b = \"2\";\nconsole.log(Number(a) + Number(b));","why":"Values from inputs and URLs are always text. Convert them first.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"jv-int-div","deck":"java","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"java","code":"System.out.println(7 / 2);","answer":"3","why":"Two `int`s give an `int`, so the .5 is dropped. Use `7.0 / 2` for 3.5.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"jv-string-plus","deck":"java","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"java","code":"System.out.println(\"Total: \" + 1 + 2);","answer":"Total: 12","why":"It works left to right. Once there's text, `+` joins text. Use brackets: `(1 + 2)`.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"jv-string-plus-2","deck":"java","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"java","code":"System.out.println(1 + 2 + \" total\");","answer":"3 total","why":"Left to right again: `1 + 2` is number maths, and then it joins the text.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"jv-string-equals","deck":"java","kind":"predict","ask":"A classic trap. What does this print?","lang":"java","code":"String a = new String(\"hi\");\nString b = new String(\"hi\");\nSystem.out.println(a == b);\nSystem.out.println(a.equals(b));","answer":"false\ntrue","why":"`==` checks whether they're the same object. `.equals` compares the text. Always use `.equals` for strings.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"jv-increment","deck":"java","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"java","code":"int n = 5;\nSystem.out.println(n++);\nSystem.out.println(n);","answer":"5\n6","why":"`n++` gives the old value first, then adds one.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"jv-char-math","deck":"java","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"java","code":"System.out.println('a' + 1);","answer":"98","why":"A `char` is a number underneath, and `'a'` is 97.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"jv-for-each","deck":"java","kind":"fill","ask":"Fill the gap to go through every name.","lang":"java","code":"import java.util.List;\nList<String> names = List.of(\"Asha\", \"Ravi\");\nfor (String name ____ names) {\n    System.out.println(name);\n}","answer":":","why":"Read the `:` as \"in\".","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"jv-record","deck":"java","kind":"fill","ask":"Fill the gap to make a small data class in one line.","lang":"java","code":"____ Point(int x, int y) {}\nSystem.out.println(new Point(1, 2));","answer":"record","why":"A `record` writes the constructor, getters, `equals` and `toString` for you.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"jv-stream","deck":"java","kind":"write","ask":"Using a stream, make a list of only the names longer than 3 letters.","lang":"java","code":"import java.util.List;\nList<String> names = List.of(\"Asha\", \"Mei\", \"Ravi\");","answerLang":"java","answer":"import java.util.List;\nList<String> names = List.of(\"Asha\", \"Mei\", \"Ravi\");\nList<String> longer = names.stream().filter(n -> n.length() > 3).toList();","why":"`filter` keeps the matches, and `toList` collects them.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"jv-interface","deck":"java","kind":"explain","ask":"What's an interface for?","answer":"It lists what a class promises to do, without saying how. Code can then work with anything that keeps the promise.","checked":"none"},{"id":"jv-checked","deck":"java","kind":"explain","ask":"What's a checked exception?","answer":"One the compiler makes you deal with, such as `IOException`. You must catch it or declare `throws`.","checked":"none"},{"id":"jv-primitive","deck":"java","kind":"explain","ask":"What's the difference between `int` and `Integer`?","answer":"`int` is a plain number. `Integer` is an object that wraps one. Collections like `List` need the object version.","checked":"none"},{"id":"jv-jvm","deck":"java","kind":"explain","ask":"What does `javac` make, and what runs it?","answer":"`javac` makes bytecode in `.class` files. The JVM runs that bytecode on any machine.","checked":"none"},{"id":"jv-static","deck":"java","kind":"explain","ask":"What does `static` mean?","answer":"It belongs to the class itself, not to one object. You can use it without creating an object.","checked":"none"},{"id":"jv-fix-equals","deck":"java","kind":"fix","ask":"This compiles, but can give the wrong answer. Fix it.","lang":"java","code":"String input = new String(\"yes\");\nif (input == \"yes\") {\n    System.out.println(\"Confirmed\");\n}","answerLang":"java","answer":"String input = new String(\"yes\");\nif (\"yes\".equals(input)) {\n    System.out.println(\"Confirmed\");\n}","why":"`==` compares objects, not text. Writing `\"yes\".equals(input)` also survives a null `input`.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"mf-s0c7","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"A batch job dies with S0C7. What happened, and where do you look first?","answer":"Bad data in a number field: spaces or letters reached arithmetic on a packed or zoned field. Find the failing statement and the input record, usually from a field that was never set up.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-s0c4","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"What does S0C4 mean?","answer":"The program touched memory it shouldn't, often from a bad table subscript or a CALL with mismatched parameters.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-s322","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"What does S322 mean?","answer":"The job ran out of CPU time. Often it's a loop that never ends.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-s806","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"What does S806 mean?","answer":"The program wasn't found. Check the program name and the `STEPLIB`.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-s0c7-vs-rc","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"What's the difference between an ABEND and a non-zero return code?","answer":"A return code means the step finished and is reporting how it went. An ABEND means it crashed and never finished.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-rc","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"What do return codes 0, 4, 8 and 12 usually mean?","answer":"0 is fine. 4 is a warning. 8 is an error. 12 or more is a serious error. Each shop has its own conventions.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-jcl-three","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"What are the three main JCL statements?","answer":"`JOB` names the job, `EXEC` runs a program, and `DD` connects a dataset.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-disp-shr","deck":"mainframe","kind":"fill","ask":"Fill the gap so this step reads a dataset that others can read too.","run":"none","lang":"jcl","code":"//INPUT    DD DSN=PROD.CUST.MASTER,DISP=____","answer":"SHR","why":"`SHR` shares the dataset. `OLD` asks for it alone.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-disp-new","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"What does `DISP=(NEW,CATLG,DELETE)` mean?","answer":"Create it. If the step ends well, record it in the catalog. If the step abends, delete it.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-assign","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"Why does a COBOL program say `ASSIGN TO NAMES`, not a real file name?","answer":"`NAMES` is a nickname. JCL's `//NAMES DD` says which dataset it means, so the same program can run on test or production data with no change.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-sysin","deck":"mainframe","kind":"fill","ask":"Fill the gap: which DD name does `ACCEPT` read from in batch?","run":"none","lang":"jcl","code":"//____    DD *\n20261105\n/*","answer":"SYSIN","why":"Lines under `SYSIN DD *` are in-stream data. It's a common way to pass a run date.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-file-status","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"What do FILE STATUS codes 00, 10, 23 and 35 mean?","answer":"00 is fine. 10 is end of file. 23 is record not found. 35 is file not found.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-sqlcode","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"What do SQLCODE 0, 100 and a negative number mean?","answer":"0 is fine. 100 is not found, or no more rows. Negative is an error.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-comp3","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"How many bytes does `PIC S9(5)V99 COMP-3` take?","answer":"4. It's 7 digits plus a sign, at two per byte: (7 + 1) / 2 = 4. As plain display digits, it would take 7.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-copybook","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"A copybook changes, and one program isn't recompiled. What can go wrong?","answer":"It still reads the old layout, so fields land in the wrong bytes. That's wrong data at best, and an S0C7 at worst.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-pseudo","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"What does pseudo-conversational mean in CICS?","answer":"The program sends the screen and ends. CICS starts it again when the user presses a key, so nothing holds memory while people think.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-commarea","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"If a CICS program ends after each screen, where does it keep its state?","answer":"In the COMMAREA, or a channel. CICS passes that to the next run.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-ksds","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"When would you pick a VSAM KSDS over a sequential file?","answer":"When you need to read a single record by key, without reading everything before it.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-batch-window","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"What's the batch window?","answer":"The hours when the overnight jobs must finish, before the online day begins. Missing it can hold up a business.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-ebcdic","deck":"mainframe","kind":"explain","ask":"Your sorted file comes out in a different order on the PC. Why?","answer":"Mainframes use EBCDIC, where letters sort before digits. ASCII has digits first.","checked":"none"},{"id":"mf-sort","deck":"mainframe","kind":"fill","ask":"Fill the gap to sort on the first 10 bytes as text, ascending.","run":"none","lang":"jcl","code":"//SYSIN    DD *\n  SORT FIELDS=(1,10,CH,____)\n/*","answer":"A","why":"Start, length, format, order. `A` ascending, `D` descending. `CH` is character.","checked":"none"},{"id":"py-slice","deck":"python","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"python","code":"nums = [10, 20, 30, 40]\nprint(nums[1:3])","answer":"[20, 30]","why":"A slice starts at the first position and stops before the second one.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"py-negative-index","deck":"python","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"python","code":"letters = [\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"]\nprint(letters[-1])","answer":"c","why":"Negative positions count from the end, so -1 is the last item.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"py-range","deck":"python","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"python","code":"print(list(range(2, 5)))","answer":"[2, 3, 4]","why":"`range` stops before the end number.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"py-int-div","deck":"python","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"python","code":"print(7 // 2, 7 % 2)","answer":"3 1","why":"`//` drops the remainder, and `%` gives just the remainder.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"py-mutable-default","deck":"python","kind":"predict","ask":"A classic trap. What does this print?","lang":"python","code":"def add(item, bag=[]):\n    bag.append(item)\n    return bag\n\nadd(\"a\")\nprint(add(\"b\"))","answer":"['a', 'b']","why":"A default value is made once, not on each call, so both calls share one list. Use `bag=None` and make a new list inside.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"py-list-alias","deck":"python","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"python","code":"a = [1, 2]\nb = a\nb.append(3)\nprint(a)","answer":"[1, 2, 3]","why":"`b = a` doesn't copy anything. Both names point to the same list.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"py-fstring","deck":"python","kind":"fill","ask":"Fill the gap so this prints `Hi Asha`.","lang":"python","code":"name = \"Asha\"\nprint(____\"Hi {name}\")","answer":"f","why":"An `f` before the quote turns on `{}` replacement.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"py-dict-get","deck":"python","kind":"fill","ask":"Fill the gap so a missing key gives 0, not a crash.","lang":"python","code":"stock = {\"apple\": 5}\nprint(stock.____(\"kiwi\", 0))","answer":"get","why":"`.get(key, fallback)` is the safe way to read a dictionary.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"py-comprehension","deck":"python","kind":"write","ask":"Write one line that makes `evens`: the even numbers from `nums`.","lang":"python","code":"nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]","answerLang":"python","answer":"nums = [1, 2, 3, 4]\nevens = [n for n in nums if n % 2 == 0]","why":"A list comprehension: what you want, where from, and which ones.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"py-with","deck":"python","kind":"explain","ask":"Why use `with open(...)` rather than plain `open(...)`?","answer":"It closes the file for you when the block ends, even if an error happens.","checked":"none"},{"id":"py-venv","deck":"python","kind":"explain","ask":"Why make a virtual environment for each project?","answer":"Each project gets its own libraries and versions, so one project can't break another.","checked":"none"},{"id":"py-is","deck":"python","kind":"explain","ask":"What's the difference between `==` and `is`?","answer":"`==` asks whether two values are equal. `is` asks whether they're the same object. Use `is` for `None`.","checked":"none"},{"id":"py-tuple","deck":"python","kind":"explain","ask":"What's the difference between a list and a tuple?","answer":"A list can change. A tuple can't. Use a tuple for a fixed group, such as a point `(x, y)`.","checked":"none"},{"id":"py-fix-indent","deck":"python","kind":"fix","ask":"This should print the total once, at the end. It prints a running total. Fix it.","lang":"python","code":"total = 0\nfor n in [1, 2, 3]:\n    total += n\n    print(total)","answerLang":"python","answer":"total = 0\nfor n in [1, 2, 3]:\n    total += n\nprint(total)","why":"Indentation is structure in Python. The `print` was inside the loop.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"py-fix-string-number","deck":"python","kind":"fix","ask":"This crashes. Fix it so it prints `Age: 34`.","lang":"python","code":"age = 34\nprint(\"Age: \" + age)","answerLang":"python","answer":"age = 34\nprint(f\"Age: {age}\")","why":"Python won't add text to a number. An f-string does the conversion.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"sw-range","deck":"swift","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"swift","code":"print(Array(1..<4))","answer":"[1, 2, 3]","why":"`..<` stops before the end. `...` includes it.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"sw-closed-range","deck":"swift","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"swift","code":"print(Array(1...3).count)","answer":"3","why":"`...` is a closed range, so it includes the 3.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"sw-nil-coalesce","deck":"swift","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"swift","code":"let name: String? = nil\nprint(name ?? \"guest\")","answer":"guest","why":"`??` gives a fallback when the optional is nil.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"sw-struct-copy","deck":"swift","kind":"predict","ask":"Structs are copied. What does this print?","lang":"swift","code":"struct Point { var x: Int }\n\nvar a = Point(x: 1)\nvar b = a\nb.x = 9\nprint(a.x)","answer":"1","why":"`b` is a separate copy, so changing it leaves `a` alone.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"sw-class-ref","deck":"swift","kind":"predict","ask":"Classes are shared. What does this print?","lang":"swift","code":"class Point {\n    var x: Int\n    init(x: Int) { self.x = x }\n}\n\nlet a = Point(x: 1)\nlet b = a\nb.x = 9\nprint(a.x)","answer":"9","why":"Both names point to one object. And `let` only fixes which object, not what's inside it.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"sw-int-div","deck":"swift","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"swift","code":"print(7 / 2)","answer":"3","why":"Two whole numbers give a whole number. Use `7.0 / 2.0` for 3.5.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"sw-closure","deck":"swift","kind":"predict","ask":"What does this print?","lang":"swift","code":"print([1, 2, 3].map { $0 * 2 })","answer":"[2, 4, 6]","why":"`map` makes a new list. `$0` is the current item.","checked":"ran"},{"id":"sw-if-let","deck":"swift","kind":"fill","ask":"Fill the gap to safely open this optional.","lang":"swift","code":"let nickname: String? = \"Ash\"\nif ____ nick = nickname {\n    print(nick)\n}","answer":"let","why":"`if let` runs the block only when there's a value, and gives it a safe name.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"sw-mutating","deck":"swift","kind":"fill","ask":"Fill the gap so a struct can change itself.","lang":"swift","code":"struct Counter {\n    var value = 0\n    ____ func increase() {\n        value += 1\n    }\n}","answer":"mutating","why":"Struct methods can't change the struct unless they're marked `mutating`.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"sw-guard","deck":"swift","kind":"write","ask":"Write a function `greet` that takes a `String?`. If it's nil, return \"Hi stranger\". Otherwise return \"Hi\" and the name. Use `guard`.","lang":"swift","code":"print(greet(nil))","answerLang":"swift","answer":"func greet(_ name: String?) -> String {\n    guard let name else { return \"Hi stranger\" }\n    return \"Hi \\(name)\"\n}","why":"`guard` handles the bad case first and leaves early, which keeps the main path flat.","checked":"compiled"},{"id":"sw-let-var","deck":"swift","kind":"explain","ask":"When do you use `let` and when `var`?","answer":"Use `let` by default. Change it to `var` only when the value really has to change.","checked":"none"},{"id":"sw-force","deck":"swift","kind":"explain","ask":"What does `!` do on an optional, and why be careful?","answer":"It force-opens the optional. If the value is nil, the app crashes.","checked":"none"},{"id":"sw-struct-class","deck":"swift","kind":"explain","ask":"When would you pick a class over a struct?","answer":"When several parts of the app need to share one copy, or for things like view models. Otherwise, default to a struct.","checked":"none"},{"id":"sw-state","deck":"swift","kind":"explain","ask":"What does `@State` do in SwiftUI?","answer":"It stores a value for the view. When the value changes, SwiftUI redraws the view by itself.","checked":"none"},{"id":"sw-fix-let","deck":"swift","kind":"fix","ask":"This won't compile. Fix it.","lang":"swift","code":"let count = 0\ncount += 1\nprint(count)","answerLang":"swift","answer":"var count = 0\ncount += 1\nprint(count)","why":"`let` can't change. `var` can.","checked":"compiled"}],"docs":[{"id":"variable","aka":["var","let","const"],"examples":[{"lang":"python","code":"age = 34\nage = age + 1"}],"links":[],"title":"Variable","area":"basics","simple":"A named box that holds a value.","like":"A labelled jar on a shelf. The label stays the same, and what's inside can change.","see":"variables"},{"id":"type","aka":["data type","static typing","dynamic typing"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Type","area":"basics","simple":"What kind of thing a value is: a whole number, a decimal, text, true or false.","like":"A power plug shape. A value only fits where the shape matches.","more":"Static languages (Swift, Java, TypeScript) check types before running. Dynamic ones (Python, JavaScript) find out while running.","see":"variables"},{"id":"constant","aka":["const","let","final","immutable"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Constant","area":"basics","simple":"A value that can't change after you set it.","like":"A name carved in stone. Writing it once means nobody can overwrite it by accident.","more":"Use constants by default. It makes code easier to trust, because you know the value stays put.","see":"variables"},{"id":"string","aka":["text","interpolation","f-string","template literal"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"String","area":"basics","simple":"A piece of text.","like":"Beads on a string: characters kept in order.","more":"Interpolation means dropping a value into text, such as Python's `f\"Hi {name}\"`.","see":"text"},{"id":"floating-point","aka":["float","double","0.1 + 0.2"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Floating point","area":"basics","simple":"The fast, slightly inexact way computers store decimals.","like":"Writing 1/3 as 0.333. Useful, but never quite right.","more":"Fine for graphics and science. Not for money.","see":"numbers"},{"id":"decimal-type","aka":["Decimal","BigDecimal","money"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Exact decimals","area":"basics","simple":"A number type that keeps every digit exactly, used for money.","like":"Counting coins rather than estimating the weight of the pile.","see":"numbers"},{"id":"boolean","aka":["bool","true","false"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Boolean","area":"basics","simple":"A value that's only ever true or false.","like":"A light switch.","see":"if"},{"id":"condition","aka":["if","else","switch"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Condition","area":"basics","simple":"A yes/no question your code asks before choosing what to do.","like":"A fork in the road, with a sign that says which way to go.","see":"if"},{"id":"loop","aka":["for","while","iteration","PERFORM"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Loop","area":"basics","simple":"Code that runs again and again until told to stop.","like":"Doing laps until the coach blows the whistle.","more":"If the stop condition never comes true, you've got an infinite loop.","see":"loops"},{"id":"array","aka":["list","array","table","OCCURS"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Array or list","area":"basics","simple":"Many values kept in order under one name.","like":"A row of numbered lockers.","see":"lists"},{"id":"index","aka":["position","zero-based","subscript"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Index","area":"basics","simple":"A value's position in a list. Most languages start at 0.","like":"Floors in a European lift: the ground floor is 0.","more":"COBOL starts at 1, and calls it a subscript.","see":"lists"},{"id":"map","aka":["dict","dictionary","hash map","object","key-value"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Map or dictionary","area":"basics","simple":"Pairs of a key and a value, so you can look things up by name.","like":"A phone book: look up the name, get the number.","see":"maps"},{"id":"hash-table","aka":["hashing"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Hash table","area":"basics","simple":"The trick behind maps: turn a key into a slot number, so finding it is instant.","like":"A cloakroom ticket. The number takes you straight to your coat.","see":"maps"},{"id":"function","aka":["method","procedure","subroutine","paragraph"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Function","area":"basics","simple":"A named piece of work you can run whenever you need it.","like":"A recipe. Write it once, cook it any time.","more":"A function that belongs to a class is called a method.","see":"functions"},{"id":"parameter","aka":["argument","input"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Parameter","area":"basics","simple":"A value you hand to a function.","like":"The ingredients you pass to the recipe.","see":"functions"},{"id":"return-value","aka":["return","output"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Return value","area":"basics","simple":"What a function hands back when it finishes.","like":"The finished dish.","see":"functions"},{"id":"class","aka":["OOP","object-oriented"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Class","area":"basics","simple":"A template for making things that have both data and behaviour.","like":"A cookie cutter. The cookies are the objects.","see":"records"},{"id":"object","aka":["instance"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Object","area":"basics","simple":"One thing made from a class, with its own values.","like":"One cookie. Same shape as the rest, with its own icing.","see":"records"},{"id":"struct","aka":["value type","record"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"Swift book","url":"https://docs.swift.org/swift-book/documentation/the-swift-programming-language/classesandstructures/"}],"title":"Struct","area":"swift","simple":"A lightweight group of values. In Swift, passing one around makes a copy.","like":"A photocopy: changing yours doesn't touch mine.","more":"Swift's default choice. Use a class when several parts of the app must share the same copy.","see":"records"},{"id":"null","aka":["nil","None","undefined","NullPointerException"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Null","area":"basics","simple":"A special value that means \"nothing here\".","like":"An empty parking space with a number on it.","more":"Using it as if it were a real value is one of the most common crashes.","see":"missing"},{"id":"exception","aka":["error","throw","try","catch","stack trace"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Exception","area":"basics","simple":"An error that stops normal work and jumps to code that can handle it.","like":"A fire alarm. Everyone stops, and the people trained for it take over.","more":"A stack trace is the list of function calls that led there. Read it from the most recent call.","see":"errors"},{"id":"file","aka":["read","write","path"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"File","area":"basics","simple":"Data saved on disk with a name, which stays when your program ends.","like":"A paper in a filing cabinet.","see":"files"},{"id":"lambda","aka":["closure","arrow function","anonymous function"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Lambda","area":"basics","simple":"A small function with no name, written right where it's needed.","like":"A sticky note with a quick instruction, rather than a whole manual.","more":"A closure is a lambda that remembers variables from around it.","see":"sort"},{"id":"library","aka":["dependency","SDK"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Library","area":"basics","simple":"Code someone else wrote that your program can use.","like":"Buying ready-made pastry rather than making your own.","more":"A framework is a bigger kind: it sets the structure and calls your code.","see":"modules"},{"id":"package","aka":["pip","npm","Maven","Gradle","Swift Package Manager"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Package manager","area":"basics","simple":"A tool that fetches libraries and keeps their versions straight.","like":"An app store for code.","more":"Python has pip, JavaScript has npm, Java has Maven and Gradle, and Swift has Swift Package Manager.","see":"modules"},{"id":"compiler","aka":["compile","build","javac","cobc","swiftc"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Compiler","area":"basics","simple":"A program that translates your code into something a machine can run, ahead of time.","like":"Translating a whole book before anyone reads it.","more":"It catches many mistakes before the program ever starts.","see":"run"},{"id":"interpreter","aka":["runtime","script"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Interpreter","area":"basics","simple":"A program that reads your code and runs it as it goes.","like":"A live translator, working sentence by sentence.","see":"run"},{"id":"async","aka":["await","promise","future","concurrency"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Async","area":"basics","simple":"Starting slow work without freezing everything while you wait.","like":"Ordering at a cafe, and sitting down until your name is called.","see":"async"},{"id":"thread","aka":["parallel","multithreading","race condition"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Thread","area":"basics","simple":"One line of work inside a program. Several can run at the same time.","like":"Several cooks in one kitchen. Faster, until two reach for the same pan.","more":"That clash is called a race condition.","see":"async"},{"id":"database","aka":["table","row","Postgres","SQLite","MySQL"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Database","area":"basics","simple":"Software that stores data safely and answers questions about it.","like":"A very fast librarian who never loses anything.","see":"database"},{"id":"sql","aka":["query","SELECT","JOIN"],"examples":[{"lang":"sql","code":"SELECT name, balance\nFROM customer\nWHERE balance > 100\nORDER BY name;"}],"links":[],"title":"SQL","area":"basics","simple":"The language for asking a database for data.","like":"Filling in a very precise request form.","see":"database"},{"id":"sql-injection","aka":["parameterized query","placeholder"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"SQL injection","area":"basics","simple":"An attack where someone types SQL into a form, and your program runs it.","like":"A note saying \"and also give me the safe\", which the clerk obeys.","more":"The fix is simple: always use placeholders, never paste input into SQL.","see":"database"},{"id":"validation","aka":["input checking","sanitize"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Validation","area":"basics","simple":"Checking that what someone gave you is sensible before you use it.","like":"A bouncer checking IDs at the door.","see":"input"},{"id":"api","aka":["interface","endpoint","REST"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"API","area":"web","simple":"A way for one program to ask another for something.","like":"A waiter. You don't go into the kitchen: you order, and the food comes back."},{"id":"http","aka":["GET","POST","status code","404","HTTPS"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"HTTP","area":"web","simple":"The language web browsers and servers use to talk.","like":"Posting a letter and getting one back. Each has a status: 200 is fine, 404 is not found."},{"id":"json","aka":["data format"],"examples":[{"lang":"json","code":"{ \"name\": \"Asha\", \"balance\": 150 }"}],"links":[],"title":"JSON","area":"web","simple":"A plain-text way to write down data, used by nearly every API.","like":"A form anyone can read, in any language."},{"id":"frontend-backend","aka":["client","server","full stack"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Frontend and backend","area":"web","simple":"The frontend is what you see in the browser. The backend runs on a server behind it.","like":"A restaurant: the dining room, and the kitchen behind the wall."},{"id":"git","aka":["version control","commit","branch","GitHub"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Git","area":"web","simple":"A tool that saves the history of your code, so you can go back and work safely.","like":"Save points in a game. Branches let you try a risky path."},{"id":"unit-test","aka":["test","pytest","JUnit","XCTest"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Unit test","area":"basics","simple":"A small piece of code that checks another piece works.","like":"A smoke alarm test. It takes a second, and you only care when it fails."},{"id":"env-var","aka":[".env","secret","API key"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Environment variable","area":"web","simple":"A setting that lives outside your code, often a password or key.","like":"A key under the mat, not written on the front door.","more":"Never put secrets in your code or commit them to Git."},{"id":"terminal","aka":["command line","shell","bash","zsh"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Terminal","area":"web","simple":"A window where you type commands instead of clicking.","like":"Talking to your computer rather than pointing at things."},{"id":"list-comprehension","aka":[],"examples":[{"lang":"python","code":"big = [p for p in prices if p > 10]"}],"links":[{"label":"Python tutorial","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/tutorial/datastructures.html#list-comprehensions"}],"title":"List comprehension","area":"python","simple":"A one-line way to build a list from another list.","like":"Saying \"the red ones from that pile\" rather than sorting by hand.","see":"sort"},{"id":"venv","aka":["venv","pip install"],"examples":[{"lang":"bash","code":"python3 -m venv .venv\nsource .venv/bin/activate\npip install requests"}],"links":[{"label":"Python docs","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/library/venv.html"}],"title":"Virtual environment","area":"python","simple":"A private folder of libraries for one project.","like":"A separate toolbox per job, so tools don't get mixed up."},{"id":"decorator","aka":["@"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"Python glossary","url":"https://docs.python.org/3/glossary.html#term-decorator"}],"title":"Decorator","area":"python","simple":"A line starting with `@` that wraps extra behaviour around a function or class.","like":"Gift wrap. Same thing inside, with something added around it."},{"id":"optional","aka":["?","if let","guard let","!","unwrap"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"Swift book","url":"https://docs.swift.org/swift-book/documentation/the-swift-programming-language/thebasics/#Optionals"}],"title":"Optional","area":"swift","simple":"A type that says \"this might be nil\", so you must check before using it.","like":"A wrapped box that might be empty. You open it before you use what's inside.","more":"`!` force-opens the box, and crashes if it's empty. Use it rarely.","see":"missing"},{"id":"swiftui","aka":["View","body","modifier"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"Apple docs","url":"https://developer.apple.com/documentation/swiftui"}],"title":"SwiftUI","area":"swift","simple":"Apple's way to build screens by describing them in Swift.","like":"Telling a designer what you want, rather than drawing it yourself.","see":"page"},{"id":"protocol","aka":["interface","conformance"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"Swift book","url":"https://docs.swift.org/swift-book/documentation/the-swift-programming-language/protocols/"}],"title":"Protocol","area":"swift","simple":"A list of what a type promises to be able to do. Java and TypeScript call it an interface.","like":"A job description. Anyone who meets it can do the job."},{"id":"xcode","aka":["simulator","iOS"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"Apple developer","url":"https://developer.apple.com/xcode/"}],"title":"Xcode","area":"swift","simple":"Apple's app for building iPhone and Mac apps. You need it for full SwiftUI work.","like":"The workshop that holds every tool for Apple apps."},{"id":"jvm","aka":["Java Virtual Machine","bytecode","JDK"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"Learn Java","url":"https://dev.java/learn/"}],"title":"JVM","area":"java","simple":"The program that runs Java. Java compiles to bytecode, and the JVM runs that anywhere.","like":"A universal adapter, so one plug works in any country.","see":"run"},{"id":"generics","aka":["<T>","List<String>"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"Learn Java","url":"https://dev.java/learn/generics/"}],"title":"Generics","area":"java","simple":"A way to say \"a list of what?\", so the compiler can check what goes in.","like":"A labelled bin: \"paper only\".","more":"The same idea appears in Swift and TypeScript."},{"id":"maven","aka":["pom.xml","build.gradle"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"Maven","url":"https://maven.apache.org/"}],"title":"Maven and Gradle","area":"java","simple":"Java's tools for fetching libraries and building your project.","like":"A project manager who collects the parts and runs the assembly line."},{"id":"spring","aka":["Spring","framework"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"Spring Boot","url":"https://spring.io/projects/spring-boot"}],"title":"Spring Boot","area":"java","simple":"The most popular way to build Java web services.","like":"A fully fitted kitchen, rather than bare walls."},{"id":"dom","aka":["document","querySelector"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"MDN","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Document_Object_Model"}],"title":"DOM","area":"frontend","simple":"The browser's live model of the page. JavaScript changes the page by changing it.","like":"A family tree of everything on the page.","see":"page"},{"id":"event","aka":["click","addEventListener","handler"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"MDN","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Core/Scripting/Events"}],"title":"Event","area":"frontend","simple":"A signal that something happened, such as a click.","like":"A doorbell. Your code is the person who answers.","see":"click"},{"id":"state","aka":["useState","@State"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"State","area":"frontend","simple":"What your app remembers right now.","like":"A scoreboard. Change the numbers and everyone sees the new score.","see":"click"},{"id":"component","aka":["React","Vue","Svelte","View"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"React docs","url":"https://react.dev/learn"}],"title":"Component","area":"frontend","simple":"A reusable piece of screen, with its own look and logic.","like":"A Lego brick.","see":"page"},{"id":"box-model","aka":["margin","padding","border"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"MDN","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Core/Styling_basics/Box_model"}],"title":"Box model","area":"frontend","simple":"Every element is a box: content, then padding, then border, then margin.","like":"A framed picture: the picture, the mount, the frame, and the gap on the wall.","see":"layout"},{"id":"flexbox","aka":["display flex","gap","grid"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"MDN","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Core/CSS_layout/Flexbox"}],"title":"Flexbox","area":"frontend","simple":"CSS for lining things up in a row or column.","like":"Books on a shelf that shuffle along to fit.","more":"Use flexbox for a line of things, and CSS grid for rows and columns together.","see":"layout"},{"id":"responsive","aka":["media query","mobile"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"MDN","url":"https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Learn_web_development/Core/CSS_layout/Responsive_Design"}],"title":"Responsive design","area":"frontend","simple":"Making one page look right on any screen size.","like":"Water taking the shape of its glass.","see":"layout"},{"id":"npm","aka":["node_modules","package.json","bundler","Vite"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"npm docs","url":"https://docs.npmjs.com/"}],"title":"npm","area":"frontend","simple":"JavaScript's package manager. `package.json` lists what a project needs.","like":"A shopping list, with a shop that delivers."},{"id":"typescript","aka":["tsc","types"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"TypeScript handbook","url":"https://www.typescriptlang.org/docs/handbook/intro.html"}],"title":"TypeScript","area":"frontend","simple":"JavaScript with types added. It catches mistakes before the code runs.","like":"Spell-check for your code.","more":"The types are removed when it runs, so browsers just see JavaScript."},{"id":"mainframe","aka":["z/OS","IBM Z","big iron"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"IBM z/OS docs","url":"https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/zos"}],"title":"Mainframe","area":"mainframe","simple":"A very large, very reliable computer. Banks, airlines and governments run on them.","like":"A freight train. Not the fastest off the line, but it moves everything, all day, without stopping.","more":"z/OS is the main operating system. It's built for huge volumes of work, and for never going down."},{"id":"batch-online","aka":["batch job","overnight","OLTP","transaction"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Batch and online","area":"mainframe","simple":"Batch is big work done in bulk with nobody watching. Online is small work done live for a person.","like":"Batch is a laundry service. Online is a coffee counter.","more":"Banks take payments online all day, then run batch overnight to settle everything.","see":"run"},{"id":"jcl","aka":["Job Control Language","JOB","EXEC","DD"],"examples":[{"lang":"jcl","code":"//MYJOB    JOB (ACCT),'REPORT',CLASS=A,MSGCLASS=X\n//STEP1    EXEC PGM=REPORT1\n//INPUT    DD DSN=PROD.SALES.DAILY,DISP=SHR\n//OUTPUT   DD SYSOUT=*"}],"links":[{"label":"IBM JCL reference","url":"https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/zos/latest?topic=mvs-zos-jcl-reference"}],"title":"JCL","area":"mainframe","simple":"The instructions that tell z/OS which programs to run and which files to give them.","like":"A work order pinned to a job: which machine, which materials, where the result goes.","more":"Three main statements: `JOB` starts it, `EXEC` runs a program, `DD` connects a file.","see":"run"},{"id":"dd-statement","aka":["DDNAME","DISP","ASSIGN"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"DD statement","area":"mainframe","simple":"A JCL line that connects a nickname in your program to a real dataset.","like":"A label on a plug socket. The program plugs in to `INPUT`, and JCL says what's wired behind it.","more":"`DISP=SHR` means read and share. `DISP=OLD` means I need it alone. `DISP=(NEW,CATLG)` means create it and record it.","see":"files"},{"id":"dataset","aka":["DSN","sequential file","QSAM","LRECL","RECFM"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Dataset","area":"mainframe","simple":"The mainframe word for a file. It's made of fixed-size records, not lines.","like":"A deck of cards, all exactly the same size.","more":"`LRECL` is the record length, often 80 from punched cards. `RECFM=FB` means fixed length, blocked. Names look like `PROD.CUST.MASTER`.","see":"files"},{"id":"pds","aka":["PDSE","partitioned dataset","member","library"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"PDS","area":"mainframe","simple":"A dataset that holds many small members. It works like a folder.","like":"A folder of documents, stored as one big file.","more":"Your COBOL source, copybooks and JCL all usually live in these."},{"id":"vsam","aka":["KSDS","ESDS","RRDS","IDCAMS","indexed file"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"IBM VSAM guide","url":"https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/zos/latest?topic=dfsms-zos-using-data-sets"}],"title":"VSAM","area":"mainframe","simple":"A file you can jump into by key, instead of reading from the top.","like":"A book with an index, rather than a scroll.","more":"A KSDS is keyed, the one you'll use most. Set one up with the IDCAMS utility.","see":"maps"},{"id":"pic-clause","aka":["PICTURE","PIC X","PIC 9","V","S","Z"],"examples":[{"lang":"cobol","code":"       01  WS-NAME    PIC X(20).\n       01  WS-TOTAL   PIC S9(7)V99.\n       01  WS-OUT     PIC ZZZ,ZZ9.99."}],"links":[],"title":"PIC clause","area":"mainframe","simple":"A template that says exactly what a COBOL field holds, one character at a time.","like":"A form with one box per letter.","more":"`X` any character, `9` a digit, `V` an invisible decimal point, `S` a sign, `Z` a digit that shows as a space when it's a leading zero.","see":"variables"},{"id":"level-number","aka":["01","05","77","88"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Level numbers","area":"mainframe","simple":"The numbers in front of COBOL fields that show what sits inside what.","like":"Outline numbering: a heading, with sub-points under it.","more":"`01` is a whole record. Higher numbers go inside it. `88` isn't a field: it names a value.","see":"records"},{"id":"group-item","aka":["record","elementary item","REDEFINES"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Group item","area":"mainframe","simple":"A COBOL field made up of smaller fields.","like":"An address, made up of street, city and postcode.","more":"Moving a group copies its bytes as one chunk, with no conversions. `REDEFINES` lets you view the same bytes a second way.","see":"records"},{"id":"88-level","aka":["condition name","SET TO TRUE"],"examples":[{"lang":"cobol","code":"       01  WS-STATUS   PIC X.\n           88  ACTIVE  VALUE \"A\".\n           88  CLOSED  VALUE \"C\"."}],"links":[],"title":"88 level","area":"mainframe","simple":"A name for a value of a field, so your code reads like English.","like":"Naming a switch position: \"on\" instead of \"1\".","see":"missing"},{"id":"comp-3","aka":["COMP-3","COMP","binary","zoned decimal"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Packed decimal","area":"mainframe","simple":"A compact, exact way to store numbers: two digits per byte, with a sign at the end.","like":"Writing money in a ledger, not estimating it.","more":"`COMP-3` is the usual choice for money. `COMP` is binary, used for counters. Plain `PIC 9` is zoned: one byte per digit.","see":"numbers"},{"id":"copybook","aka":["COPY","member","record layout"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Copybook","area":"mainframe","simple":"A shared piece of COBOL, usually a record layout, that programs pull in with `COPY`.","like":"A standard form template that every department uses.","more":"Change a copybook and every program using it needs recompiling.","see":"modules"},{"id":"paragraph","aka":["PERFORM","section","GO TO"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Paragraph","area":"mainframe","simple":"A named block of COBOL that you run with `PERFORM`.","like":"A function with no inputs, sharing all the same memory.","see":"functions"},{"id":"call-subprogram","aka":["subprogram","USING","LINKAGE SECTION"],"examples":[{"lang":"cobol","code":"           CALL \"TAXCALC\" USING WS-PRICE WS-TAX."}],"links":[],"title":"CALL and LINKAGE","area":"mainframe","simple":"How one COBOL program runs another, passing data by sharing memory.","like":"Lending a colleague your notebook. Whatever they write in it, you'll see.","see":"functions"},{"id":"ebcdic","aka":["ASCII","code page"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"EBCDIC","area":"mainframe","simple":"The mainframe's character encoding. It's different from the ASCII used everywhere else.","like":"Two alphabets with the same letters in a different order.","more":"In EBCDIC, letters sort before digits, and lowercase before uppercase. In ASCII, it's the other way round. That catches people out in file transfers."},{"id":"abend","aka":["abnormal end","S0C7","S0C4","S322","S806","crash"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"ABEND","area":"mainframe","simple":"A job or program crashing on a mainframe.","like":"The job falling over, with a code saying why.","more":"S0C7: bad data in a number field. S0C4: illegal memory access. S322: ran out of CPU time. S806: program not found.","see":"errors"},{"id":"return-code","aka":["RC","COND","condition code","MAXCC","IF THEN"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Return code","area":"mainframe","simple":"A number each step leaves behind: 0 is fine, 4 is a warning, 8 and up means problems.","like":"A traffic light at the end of each step.","more":"JCL uses it to decide what runs next. Set it from COBOL with `MOVE 8 TO RETURN-CODE`."},{"id":"file-status","aka":["00","10","23","35"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"FILE STATUS","area":"mainframe","simple":"A two-character code your program checks after each file action.","like":"A note saying how the last errand went.","more":"`00` fine, `10` end of file, `23` record not found, `35` file not found.","see":"files"},{"id":"sort-step","aka":["SORT","ICETOOL","SORTIN","SORTOUT"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"DFSORT","area":"mainframe","simple":"A utility that sorts, filters and merges huge files with no COBOL.","like":"A hired sorting machine. Feed it the rules and the file.","see":"sort"},{"id":"db2","aka":["EXEC SQL","SQLCODE","SQLCA","DCLGEN","BIND"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"IBM Db2 docs","url":"https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/db2-for-zos"}],"title":"Db2","area":"mainframe","simple":"IBM's relational database. COBOL talks to it with SQL embedded in the program.","like":"The same SQL as elsewhere, written inside your COBOL.","more":"After each statement, check `SQLCODE`: 0 is fine, 100 is not found, negative is an error.","see":"database"},{"id":"cics","aka":["transaction","EXEC CICS","COMMAREA","pseudo-conversational"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"IBM CICS docs","url":"https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/cics-ts"}],"title":"CICS","area":"mainframe","simple":"The system that runs online mainframe programs for thousands of users at once.","like":"A maitre d' who seats everyone, then only looks after a table when it's needed.","more":"Programs end after sending a screen, so one system can serve huge numbers of people.","see":"page"},{"id":"bms","aka":["map","mapset","3270","green screen"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"BMS","area":"mainframe","simple":"The way you define a 3270 screen layout for CICS.","like":"A stencil laid over a 24-by-80 grid.","see":"layout"},{"id":"ispf","aka":["green screen","editor","3.4"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"TSO and ISPF","area":"mainframe","simple":"How you log in and work on a mainframe: browsing datasets, editing code, submitting jobs.","like":"The mainframe's desktop and text editor, all in a terminal.","more":"ISPF option 3.4 lists datasets. `SUB` submits the JCL you're editing."},{"id":"sdsf","aka":["spool","SYSOUT","job output","MAXCC"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"SDSF","area":"mainframe","simple":"The screen where you watch jobs run and read their output.","like":"The tracking page for a parcel.","more":"Job output waits in the spool. The first thing to look at is the return code, then the SYSOUT."},{"id":"scheduler","aka":["Control-M","TWS","CA-7","batch window"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"Job scheduler","area":"mainframe","simple":"Software that runs thousands of jobs in the right order, night after night.","like":"An air traffic controller for jobs.","more":"The batch window is the time they have to finish before the online day begins."},{"id":"racf","aka":["security","ACF2","Top Secret"],"examples":[],"links":[],"title":"RACF","area":"mainframe","simple":"The mainframe's security system: who can use what.","like":"A guard with a list of who may enter each room."},{"id":"zowe","aka":["VS Code","modern mainframe","z/OS Connect"],"examples":[],"links":[{"label":"Zowe","url":"https://www.zowe.org/"}],"title":"Zowe","area":"mainframe","simple":"Open-source tools that let you work on a mainframe from VS Code and a normal terminal.","like":"A modern front door on an old, solid building."},{"id":"learn-mainframe","aka":["GnuCOBOL","practice"],"examples":[{"lang":"bash","code":"cobc -x hello.cob\n./hello"}],"links":[{"label":"Free COBOL course","url":"https://github.com/openmainframeproject/cobol-programming-course"},{"label":"GnuCOBOL","url":"https://gnucobol.sourceforge.io/"}],"title":"Practising without a mainframe","area":"mainframe","simple":"GnuCOBOL runs COBOL on your own Mac. For real JCL, you need access to a z/OS system.","like":"Learning to drive in a car park before the motorway.","more":"The Open Mainframe Project's free COBOL course includes hands-on labs."}]};
